1
00:00:01,001 --> 00:00:02,993
♪♪

2
00:01:05,265 --> 00:01:07,105
Quiet, Mr. Matlock,

3
00:01:07,134 --> 00:01:08,397
or I'll hold you in contempt.

4
00:01:11,672 --> 00:01:14,267
No, that's not all.
Where were you...

5
00:01:14,408 --> 00:01:15,706
Objection, Your Honor.

6
00:01:15,842 --> 00:01:18,937
Mr. Matlock is engaging
in absurd speculation,

7
00:01:19,079 --> 00:01:24,143
and then using that speculation to
support his unfounded accusation.

8
00:01:24,284 --> 00:01:27,186
If client is guilty...

9
00:01:27,321 --> 00:01:29,813
We're talking about murder...

10
00:01:29,957 --> 00:01:31,000
- No!
- Overruled.

11
00:01:31,024 --> 00:01:33,425
Order! Mr. Matlock, you
must restrain yourself.

12
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:36,760
- We, the jury, find the defendant guilty.
- This is all speculation, Your Honor!

13
00:01:36,863 --> 00:01:40,129
- We find the defendant not guilty.
- I object. The witness is not on trial.

14
00:01:40,267 --> 00:01:42,065
Mr. Matlock, you
are in contempt.

15
00:01:42,202 --> 00:01:43,780
- Your Honor, I object.
- Bailiff, you are hereby instructed...

16
00:01:43,804 --> 00:01:45,932
to remove Mr. Matlock from
this courtroom immediately.

17
00:01:46,073 --> 00:01:47,650
The witness is
fabricating the entire...

18
00:01:47,674 --> 00:01:49,973
Mr. Matlock, you are
incapable of sticking to the facts

19
00:01:50,110 --> 00:01:52,102
and refraining from
these courtroom theatrics!

20
00:01:52,245 --> 00:01:54,223
Quiet! I'll hold
you in contempt!

21
00:01:54,247 --> 00:01:56,011
- That's enough!
- Do you understand?

22
00:01:56,149 --> 00:01:58,516
That's enough, Mr. Matlock.

23
00:03:13,827 --> 00:03:16,387
I told the Burkes you had
that appointment at 5:00,

24
00:03:16,530 --> 00:03:19,261
so they're just going to meet us
at the restaurant, if that's okay?

25
00:03:19,399 --> 00:03:21,425
Fine, fine.

26
00:03:21,568 --> 00:03:23,400
I have an appointment
at 5:00 tonight?

27
00:03:23,537 --> 00:03:24,732
- Friday night.
- Oh.

28
00:03:24,871 --> 00:03:26,362
I've got a new client.

29
00:03:26,506 --> 00:03:29,670
I promised I'd meet him at
Stumpy Point after we adjourn here.

30
00:03:29,810 --> 00:03:32,939
You're not going to drive all the
way to Stumpy Point this afternoon?

31
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:34,776
It's just Stumpy Point.

32
00:03:34,915 --> 00:03:37,510
It's only 9:00 in the morning,

33
00:03:37,651 --> 00:03:40,086
and you can't even open
your mouth without yawning.

34
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:41,381
Mmm.

35
00:03:41,521 --> 00:03:43,820
Has it occurred to
you that you might be

36
00:03:43,957 --> 00:03:46,426
spreading yourself a
little thin these days?

37
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:51,589
Oh, it's just against my nature
to turn down work, you know?

38
00:03:51,732 --> 00:03:54,292
Mmm.

39
00:03:54,434 --> 00:03:56,494
You're cutting it a little
close, aren't you, Ben?

40
00:03:56,636 --> 00:03:58,571
Huh?

41
00:03:58,705 --> 00:04:01,140
This isn't a business
deal that can go sour, Ben.

42
00:04:01,274 --> 00:04:03,300
I can't afford to lose here.

43
00:04:03,443 --> 00:04:04,741
Oh, Frank,

44
00:04:04,878 --> 00:04:08,542
I've been doing this so long
I've got it down to a science.

45
00:04:08,682 --> 00:04:12,517
It takes me exactly 20 minutes to
drive from my house to the parking lot,

46
00:04:12,652 --> 00:04:15,554
two minutes to walk from the
parking lot to the courtroom,

47
00:04:15,689 --> 00:04:18,318
nine seconds to
organize my papers.

48
00:04:18,458 --> 00:04:19,926
All rise.

49
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:24,589
And about two seconds longer
to get to my feet than it used to.

50
00:04:26,099 --> 00:04:27,829
Thank you, Lieutenant.

51
00:04:27,968 --> 00:04:29,561
No more questions.

52
00:04:29,703 --> 00:04:30,703
Mr. Matlock?

53
00:04:30,771 --> 00:04:32,171
Thank you, Your Honor.

54
00:04:32,305 --> 00:04:35,673
Uh, Lieutenant, were
you able to find anybody

55
00:04:35,809 --> 00:04:38,643
who saw Mr. Englewood
leave the bakery that day?

56
00:04:38,779 --> 00:04:40,042
Who?

57
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:41,773
My client, Mr. Englewood.

58
00:04:41,915 --> 00:04:44,384
You mean Mr. Mitchell.

59
00:04:45,485 --> 00:04:46,817
Uh...

60
00:04:46,953 --> 00:04:48,683
Yeah. Yeah, Mr. Mitchell.
What did I say?

61
00:04:48,822 --> 00:04:50,051
Englewood.

62
00:04:50,190 --> 00:04:52,091
Nah, I... I meant Mr. Mitchell.

63
00:04:53,126 --> 00:04:55,095
In answer to your question, no.

64
00:04:55,228 --> 00:04:57,527
We didn't find anyone who
saw him leave the bakery.

65
00:04:57,664 --> 00:04:59,599
Thank you, Lieutenant.

66
00:05:03,670 --> 00:05:05,502
Remember me?

67
00:05:05,639 --> 00:05:07,039
Frank Mitchell?

68
00:05:07,174 --> 00:05:08,174
Yeah.

69
00:05:08,241 --> 00:05:12,406
I opened my mouth to say your name,
and another client's name popped out.

70
00:05:12,546 --> 00:05:15,641
Try not to lose interest
in my case, will you?

71
00:05:19,085 --> 00:05:21,145
Officer Taggerman, will
you please tell the court

72
00:05:21,288 --> 00:05:24,156
what you found when you
searched the defendant's garage?

73
00:05:24,291 --> 00:05:25,816
Objection.

74
00:05:25,959 --> 00:05:27,689
The warrant Officer
Taggerman had

75
00:05:27,828 --> 00:05:30,855
when he searched my
client's garage was invalid.

76
00:05:30,997 --> 00:05:33,626
It was issued without
probable cause.

77
00:05:33,767 --> 00:05:36,896
Anything seized in that
search is therefore inadmissible.

78
00:05:37,037 --> 00:05:39,063
But Officer Taggerman
didn't know that.

79
00:05:39,206 --> 00:05:40,799
He believed the
warrant was valid.

80
00:05:40,941 --> 00:05:42,773
The warrant was invalid.

81
00:05:42,909 --> 00:05:45,310
Who cares what Officer
Taggerman believed?

82
00:05:45,445 --> 00:05:49,644
Well, the United States
Supreme Court does.

83
00:05:49,783 --> 00:05:54,444
According to <i>United
States vs. Leon,</i>

84
00:05:54,588 --> 00:05:57,023
good faith belief
justifies a search

85
00:05:57,157 --> 00:05:59,388
whether the warrant
is valid or not.

86
00:05:59,526 --> 00:06:02,428
The United States vs. who?

87
00:06:02,562 --> 00:06:03,894
Leon.

88
00:06:04,030 --> 00:06:05,862
When did this happen?

89
00:06:05,999 --> 00:06:07,695
Late last year.

90
00:06:07,834 --> 00:06:09,302
Objection overruled.

91
00:06:09,436 --> 00:06:11,769
The gun seized by
Officer Taggerman

92
00:06:11,905 --> 00:06:14,136
will be admitted into evidence.

93
00:06:16,576 --> 00:06:18,374
What is going on, Ben?

94
00:06:18,511 --> 00:06:20,503
Oh, I... I got overruled.

95
00:06:21,948 --> 00:06:24,281
It's not the end of the world.

96
00:06:25,318 --> 00:06:27,446
We've got to talk.

97
00:06:30,223 --> 00:06:34,251
Things will look better once we start
presenting our case, I guarantee it.

98
00:06:34,394 --> 00:06:36,886
You just have to hang on.

99
00:06:37,030 --> 00:06:38,521
Ben...

100
00:06:40,567 --> 00:06:42,433
This isn't working, Ben.

101
00:06:42,569 --> 00:06:45,664
Frank... Frank, now,
you're overreacting.

102
00:06:45,805 --> 00:06:48,739
Just... I'm replacing you, Ben.

103
00:06:48,875 --> 00:06:51,743
I'm sorry, but I need an attorney
I have more confidence in.

104
00:06:51,878 --> 00:06:54,507
I'm hiring Edwin McClure.

105
00:06:55,782 --> 00:06:57,273
Edwin McClure?

106
00:06:57,417 --> 00:06:59,784
The man wins one little
case and writes a book.

107
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:01,218
You can't be serious.

108
00:07:01,354 --> 00:07:05,086
I like his approach,
and I like him.

109
00:07:05,225 --> 00:07:07,820
I feel like I can relate to
him better than I can to you.

110
00:07:07,961 --> 00:07:09,020
Relate?

111
00:07:10,764 --> 00:07:13,290
McClure and I are the same
generation. We think alike.

112
00:07:13,433 --> 00:07:17,666
Edwin McClure is
dynamic and on top of it,

113
00:07:17,804 --> 00:07:19,773
and I want him
representing my case.

114
00:07:21,508 --> 00:07:23,136
Well,

115
00:07:25,145 --> 00:07:27,205
it's your choice.

116
00:07:28,682 --> 00:07:32,380
I'll ask for a recess and
speak to Mr. McClure.

117
00:07:34,554 --> 00:07:36,022
Thanks, Ben.

118
00:07:36,156 --> 00:07:38,955
And, again, I'm sorry.

119
00:08:33,246 --> 00:08:35,147
You're withdrawing
from the case?

120
00:08:35,281 --> 00:08:36,874
Uh, that's right, Your Honor.

121
00:08:37,017 --> 00:08:39,919
I'm turning it over
to Mr. McClure here.

122
00:08:40,053 --> 00:08:41,851
But why?

123
00:08:41,988 --> 00:08:46,016
Oh, well, uh, personal reasons.

124
00:08:47,594 --> 00:08:53,090
Uh, I've... I've decided to
retire from the practice of law.

125
00:08:53,233 --> 00:08:55,361
- What?
- You have?

126
00:08:55,502 --> 00:08:58,961
Well, it, uh... It-it had
to come sometime,

127
00:08:59,105 --> 00:09:03,167
and, uh... and it just turned
out that this is a good time.

128
00:09:05,378 --> 00:09:07,643
Well, this comes
as quite a shock.

129
00:09:10,016 --> 00:09:13,145
I guess if anybody
deserves a rest, you do.

130
00:09:14,187 --> 00:09:16,452
Good luck, Ben.
You'll be missed.

131
00:09:18,558 --> 00:09:23,155
Uh, defense's motion is granted.

132
00:09:36,042 --> 00:09:40,673
♪ Softly and tenderly ♪

133
00:09:40,814 --> 00:09:45,047
♪ Jesus is calling ♪

134
00:09:45,185 --> 00:09:48,747
♪ Calling for you ♪

135
00:09:48,888 --> 00:09:52,882
♪ And for me ♪

136
00:09:53,026 --> 00:09:56,963
♪ See by the portholes ♪

137
00:09:57,097 --> 00:10:01,660
♪ He's watching and waiting ♪

138
00:10:01,801 --> 00:10:06,171
♪ Waiting for you ♪

139
00:10:06,306 --> 00:10:10,539
♪ And for me ♪

140
00:10:10,677 --> 00:10:14,079
♪ Come home ♪

141
00:10:14,214 --> 00:10:15,842
♪ Come home ♪

142
00:10:15,982 --> 00:10:18,110
♪♪

143
00:10:18,251 --> 00:10:21,085
♪ Ye who are weary ♪

144
00:10:21,221 --> 00:10:24,783
- ♪ Come home ♪
- Ben?

145
00:10:24,924 --> 00:10:26,756
- Ben?
- Huh? In here.

146
00:10:26,893 --> 00:10:29,590
Oh, hi. Hi. What's up?

147
00:10:29,729 --> 00:10:31,664
I see you're shining
your shoes there.

148
00:10:31,798 --> 00:10:33,289
Oh, yeah.

149
00:10:33,433 --> 00:10:36,665
A man can't take too
much pride in his feet.

150
00:10:37,670 --> 00:10:39,195
What's up with you?

151
00:10:39,339 --> 00:10:41,274
- What's up with us?
- Yeah.

152
00:10:41,407 --> 00:10:44,138
Well, Julie just called
and said you'd retired.

153
00:10:44,277 --> 00:10:46,405
Oh, oh, oh, oh.

154
00:10:46,546 --> 00:10:48,515
Well, yeah.

155
00:10:48,648 --> 00:10:50,378
Just like that?

156
00:10:50,517 --> 00:10:52,281
Without any
warning, you retired?

157
00:10:54,220 --> 00:10:55,882
That's right.

158
00:10:56,022 --> 00:10:58,491
Because Frank Mitchell
fired you, you retired?

159
00:10:58,625 --> 00:11:01,094
Oh, no, no, no, no.

160
00:11:01,227 --> 00:11:03,196
I just figured it was time.

161
00:11:04,264 --> 00:11:06,392
- Ben?
- Uh-huh?

162
00:11:08,067 --> 00:11:10,901
Is there something
you haven't told us?

163
00:11:11,037 --> 00:11:13,506
- Like what?
- Like, are you sick?

164
00:11:13,640 --> 00:11:16,303
No. No, no, no.

165
00:11:16,442 --> 00:11:19,537
I just figured it was time.

166
00:11:19,679 --> 00:11:22,945
And I'm leaving the office
and the practice to you.

167
00:11:23,082 --> 00:11:26,052
There'll be, uh, more
than enough work

168
00:11:26,186 --> 00:11:28,883
for both of you for a long time.

169
00:11:29,022 --> 00:11:30,991
I'm going down
tonight and get my stuff.

170
00:11:31,124 --> 00:11:33,286
- Tonight?
- Yeah.

171
00:11:33,426 --> 00:11:35,554
There's files and stuff.

172
00:11:37,096 --> 00:11:39,759
Look at that boot. Huh?

173
00:11:56,916 --> 00:11:58,384
Mmm.

174
00:12:04,023 --> 00:12:07,152
♪♪

175
00:12:15,568 --> 00:12:17,901
♪♪

176
00:12:21,407 --> 00:12:22,466
Oh, hi.

177
00:12:22,609 --> 00:12:24,703
I was waiting for
you at your house.

178
00:12:24,844 --> 00:12:26,335
I was starting to get worried.

179
00:12:26,479 --> 00:12:27,538
Well, I'm sorry.

180
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:31,014
I just thought I'd come over here
tonight and pack up some of this stuff.

181
00:12:31,150 --> 00:12:33,210
Oh, leave it here.

182
00:12:34,254 --> 00:12:36,883
I'm sure Conrad and
Michelle won't mind.

183
00:12:37,023 --> 00:12:40,152
Oh, no. They can...
They can use the space.

184
00:12:40,293 --> 00:12:42,091
See?

185
00:12:43,329 --> 00:12:45,821
♪♪

186
00:12:53,706 --> 00:12:56,608
I couldn't remember
my client's name.

187
00:12:56,743 --> 00:12:58,803
Oh, that was just
a slip of the tongue.

188
00:12:58,945 --> 00:13:00,379
It happens to everybody.

189
00:13:00,513 --> 00:13:02,607
Yeah, I guess. Yeah. Yeah.

190
00:13:03,683 --> 00:13:07,051
I didn't know about that
Supreme Court ruling.

191
00:13:07,186 --> 00:13:09,655
Well, sometimes I don't know.

192
00:13:09,789 --> 00:13:13,055
Those rulings come down so fast.

193
00:13:13,192 --> 00:13:15,161
Yeah, I guess. Yeah.

194
00:13:15,295 --> 00:13:17,821
You've just been
working too hard, that's all.

195
00:13:17,964 --> 00:13:20,763
You've got too many
cases. You're tired.

196
00:13:20,900 --> 00:13:24,462
Yeah. Yeah. Maybe.

197
00:13:26,072 --> 00:13:27,096
Or...

198
00:13:27,240 --> 00:13:30,142
Are you going to say,
"I'm not tired, I'm old"?

199
00:13:30,276 --> 00:13:33,212
No, I wasn't going to say that.

200
00:13:33,346 --> 00:13:35,542
Of course, I am.

201
00:13:35,682 --> 00:13:38,880
I mean... I mean,
not much but some.

202
00:13:39,018 --> 00:13:42,477
Hey, are we still on for Friday?

203
00:13:42,622 --> 00:13:45,956
Or are you too ancient
to take a lady to dinner?

204
00:13:46,092 --> 00:13:48,687
Oh, I'll pick you up at 6:00.

205
00:13:48,828 --> 00:13:52,890
You'll... You'll recognize
me. I'll be wearing a gray suit.

206
00:14:15,621 --> 00:14:18,022
Good morning, Miss McArdle.

207
00:14:18,157 --> 00:14:20,752
Oh, top of the morning
to you, Mr. Matlock.

208
00:14:20,893 --> 00:14:23,920
Or bottom, now that
I look at my watch.

209
00:14:24,063 --> 00:14:27,761
Well, will you be having
breakfast or lunch?

210
00:14:29,736 --> 00:14:31,705
Surprise me.

211
00:14:31,838 --> 00:14:34,137
Is this what you're
going to be doing now,

212
00:14:34,273 --> 00:14:36,765
spending half the day in bed?

213
00:14:38,878 --> 00:14:39,878
Maybe.

214
00:14:40,913 --> 00:14:43,405
Well, that's what my
Grandpa Aloysius did

215
00:14:43,549 --> 00:14:45,074
when he retired.

216
00:14:46,085 --> 00:14:49,317
He'd go to bed at
midnight, wake up at noon.

217
00:14:49,455 --> 00:14:50,889
Day in, day out.

218
00:14:51,023 --> 00:14:52,651
Sounds like the life.

219
00:14:52,792 --> 00:14:54,852
Well, he was dead in
two years, of course.

220
00:14:54,994 --> 00:14:57,725
I mean, the body can only
stand so much inactivity.

221
00:14:57,864 --> 00:15:00,459
And then there was
my second cousin Paul,

222
00:15:00,600 --> 00:15:02,728
the retired policeman.

223
00:15:02,869 --> 00:15:06,806
He shot his wife and the
family dog in a fit of rage

224
00:15:06,939 --> 00:15:09,408
and then turned
the gun on himself.

225
00:15:09,542 --> 00:15:12,239
Everyone survived.

226
00:15:12,378 --> 00:15:14,813
He was a terrible shot.

227
00:15:15,982 --> 00:15:18,281
It sounds like
retirement in your family

228
00:15:18,418 --> 00:15:20,444
is a pretty dangerous thing.

229
00:15:20,586 --> 00:15:22,316
I won't even begin to tell you

230
00:15:22,455 --> 00:15:25,220
what Uncle Bernard
did when he retired.

231
00:15:25,358 --> 00:15:28,886
I don't think I've ever heard
you speak of an Uncle Bernard.

232
00:15:29,028 --> 00:15:32,157
That's because now we
have to call him Aunt Bernice.

233
00:15:33,833 --> 00:15:35,768
Oh.

234
00:15:35,902 --> 00:15:38,736
- I still can't believe it.
- Me either.

235
00:15:38,871 --> 00:15:40,464
I guess we'll have to.

236
00:15:40,606 --> 00:15:43,235
It's not like he doesn't
deserve to retire.

237
00:15:43,376 --> 00:15:44,469
Yeah.

238
00:15:44,610 --> 00:15:47,876
I never thought about it, but I
guess we all have to retire someday.

239
00:15:48,014 --> 00:15:51,212
- I'm going to miss him.
- Well, I'm gonna see him.

240
00:15:51,350 --> 00:15:53,950
- Oh, we're all going to see him.
- We're all gonna see him, yeah.

241
00:15:54,053 --> 00:15:55,885
It just won't be the same.

242
00:15:56,022 --> 00:15:57,957
You know what?

243
00:15:58,090 --> 00:16:00,753
Let's give him a
retirement party.

244
00:16:00,893 --> 00:16:02,555
A surprise retirement party.

245
00:16:02,695 --> 00:16:03,695
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

246
00:16:03,763 --> 00:16:05,925
- What about Friday?
- That's perfect.

247
00:16:06,065 --> 00:16:07,658
He's taking me to
Lavelle's at 6:00.

248
00:16:07,800 --> 00:16:10,245
This is great. You know what?
This is going to knock his head off.

249
00:16:10,269 --> 00:16:12,069
- It'll be fun.
- We gotta get lots of people.

250
00:16:12,205 --> 00:16:14,367
And let's have something
to eat besides hot dogs.

251
00:16:14,507 --> 00:16:15,805
Anything but hot dogs.

252
00:16:15,942 --> 00:16:18,468
I've never known
what Ben sees in them.

253
00:16:42,335 --> 00:16:44,736
Oh.

254
00:17:17,904 --> 00:17:19,202
Ow!

255
00:17:30,383 --> 00:17:32,249
That's right. 6:00 at Lavelle's.

256
00:17:32,385 --> 00:17:33,762
Okay. I'll see you then.

257
00:17:33,786 --> 00:17:35,311
- Sorry I'm late.
- Hey, Julie.

258
00:17:35,454 --> 00:17:37,855
Look at all the decorations.

259
00:17:37,990 --> 00:17:39,891
We kind of got carried away.

260
00:17:40,026 --> 00:17:43,292
In fact, it might be a good idea if
you and Ben didn't show up till 6:30.

261
00:17:43,429 --> 00:17:46,331
- Give us a chance to set everything up.
- I'll give him a call.

262
00:17:46,465 --> 00:17:51,028
- How many people have you told?
- 50, and almost all of them are coming.

263
00:17:53,739 --> 00:17:55,401
He's not home.

264
00:17:55,541 --> 00:17:57,659
I sure hope he's not
on his way over here.

265
00:17:57,683 --> 00:17:58,170
Me, too.

266
00:17:58,311 --> 00:18:00,088
Want to do the dots?

267
00:18:00,112 --> 00:18:01,112
Yes.

268
00:18:01,180 --> 00:18:04,207
I'm calling my house.
Maybe he left me a message.

269
00:18:04,350 --> 00:18:07,320
You guys, that's beautiful.

270
00:18:08,654 --> 00:18:11,123
<i>Uh, Julie, it's, uh... It's Ben.</i>

271
00:18:11,257 --> 00:18:15,160
<i>Listen, I'm not going to be
able to make dinner tonight.</i>

272
00:18:15,294 --> 00:18:17,627
<i>My neighbor is mad at me,</i>

273
00:18:17,763 --> 00:18:20,460
<i>and I kind of need
some time to myself,</i>

274
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:24,765
<i>so I'm just going to leave town
and do a little fishing, okay?</i>

275
00:18:24,904 --> 00:18:26,805
<i>I'll... I'll call you
when I get back.</i>

276
00:18:26,939 --> 00:18:30,398
<i>And I'll take you to whatever
restaurant you want to go to then.</i>

277
00:18:34,313 --> 00:18:35,975
Uh-oh.

278
00:18:55,901 --> 00:18:59,030
<i>♪♪</i>

279
00:19:06,212 --> 00:19:08,272
<i>♪♪</i>

280
00:19:18,791 --> 00:19:20,453
<i>♪♪</i>

281
00:19:23,562 --> 00:19:27,158
Hello. Car trouble.

282
00:19:27,299 --> 00:19:29,393
<i>Sí</i>. Blown head gasket.

283
00:19:29,535 --> 00:19:31,970
Oh. Where are you headed?

284
00:19:32,104 --> 00:19:34,938
Oak Creek. It's
just up the road.

285
00:19:35,074 --> 00:19:37,908
I have a map. See?

286
00:19:38,044 --> 00:19:40,070
Oh. Where did you get this?

287
00:19:40,212 --> 00:19:42,078
Mr. Jones send it to me.

288
00:19:42,214 --> 00:19:43,682
He's my new boss.

289
00:19:43,816 --> 00:19:45,478
Owns a big farm by Oak Creek.

290
00:19:45,618 --> 00:19:47,610
Oh, you've got a new job.

291
00:19:47,753 --> 00:19:49,722
I have a job in Ford in Mobile.

292
00:19:49,855 --> 00:19:51,983
This one is better. More <i>dinero.</i>

293
00:19:52,124 --> 00:19:54,491
Oh. What do you do?

294
00:19:54,627 --> 00:19:55,627
Mechanic.

295
00:19:55,761 --> 00:19:59,630
I fix farm machines...
Tractors, combines, harrowbeds.

296
00:19:59,765 --> 00:20:02,667
I meet Mr. Jones at a
tractor shop in Mobile.

297
00:20:02,802 --> 00:20:05,772
He see I do good work,
promised to pay me double.

298
00:20:05,905 --> 00:20:07,897
- Oh.
- But this is big problem.

299
00:20:08,040 --> 00:20:09,736
Yeah.

300
00:20:09,875 --> 00:20:13,573
Well, I tell you what, get
your stuff and I'll give you a lift.

301
00:20:13,713 --> 00:20:16,080
You're a very nice man.

302
00:20:18,451 --> 00:20:20,613
Oak Creek. Yeah.
Yeah. Okay. Here.

303
00:20:20,753 --> 00:20:23,416
Here. I'll help you get this...

304
00:20:23,556 --> 00:20:26,856
- You live in Oak Creek?
- Oh, no. Atlanta.

305
00:20:26,992 --> 00:20:28,688
They have farms there?

306
00:20:28,828 --> 00:20:31,229
Well, no... no,
not right in town.

307
00:20:31,363 --> 00:20:34,925
I'll tell you what they do
have, though... lawyers.

308
00:20:35,067 --> 00:20:37,229
That's what I was... a lawyer.

309
00:20:37,369 --> 00:20:38,369
Retired.

310
00:20:38,437 --> 00:20:40,338
Now you fish.

311
00:20:40,473 --> 00:20:42,237
Yeah. Ben Matlock.

312
00:20:42,374 --> 00:20:45,139
I'm Roman Elonzo
Comaneros Lomas.

313
00:20:45,277 --> 00:20:46,905
- Ramon.
- Ramon.

314
00:20:47,046 --> 00:20:48,412
You like to fish?

315
00:20:48,547 --> 00:20:50,243
I'm going to learn to like it.

316
00:20:50,382 --> 00:20:53,978
You have time to do now what you
want to do, not what you have to do.

317
00:20:54,120 --> 00:20:56,316
You are a lucky man.

318
00:20:57,790 --> 00:21:00,350
Well, you're young,

319
00:21:00,493 --> 00:21:04,294
got your life ahead of
you, got a brand-new job.

320
00:21:04,430 --> 00:21:06,422
You're the lucky one, Ramon.

321
00:21:08,367 --> 00:21:10,700
Where did you learn about
all this farm equipment?

322
00:21:10,836 --> 00:21:12,896
- Back home in El Salvador.
- Uh-huh.

323
00:21:13,038 --> 00:21:15,371
My family is still there.

324
00:21:17,276 --> 00:21:21,179
Here is my mother, my
two sisters and my brothers.

325
00:21:21,313 --> 00:21:22,804
- You're the oldest?
<i>- Sí.</i>

326
00:21:22,948 --> 00:21:24,849
I send all my money to them.

327
00:21:24,984 --> 00:21:26,976
- Someday they live here, too.
- Ah.

328
00:21:27,119 --> 00:21:29,054
Fasten your seatbelt.

329
00:22:16,669 --> 00:22:17,728
- Fill her up?
- Yeah.

330
00:22:17,870 --> 00:22:19,930
- I come back for my things.
- Okay.

331
00:22:20,072 --> 00:22:22,234
Nice day.

332
00:22:22,374 --> 00:22:24,866
Elbow aches. Gonna rain.

333
00:22:25,010 --> 00:22:26,410
Mmm.

334
00:22:26,545 --> 00:22:30,209
Oh. Whereabouts
is the, uh, uh...

335
00:22:30,349 --> 00:22:32,875
- Right there.
- Okay, thanks.

336
00:22:40,192 --> 00:22:42,718
Hi. My truck broke
down on the road.

337
00:22:42,862 --> 00:22:45,127
I need for someone
to bring it here.

338
00:22:45,264 --> 00:22:48,029
- So you need a tow?
<i>- Sí.</i>

339
00:22:50,569 --> 00:22:52,561
Name?

340
00:22:52,705 --> 00:22:55,174
Ramon Comaneros.

341
00:22:55,307 --> 00:22:58,937
Spell it, slowly.

342
00:22:59,078 --> 00:23:01,172
C-O...

343
00:23:05,117 --> 00:23:06,710
What you got on that rag?

344
00:23:06,852 --> 00:23:09,412
- Ammonia on it.
- It smells awful.

345
00:23:09,555 --> 00:23:11,581
Don't streak, don't run,

346
00:23:11,724 --> 00:23:15,024
cheaper than dirt and
cleans my fingernails.

347
00:23:15,160 --> 00:23:17,789
14.55.

348
00:23:19,431 --> 00:23:21,263
Okay.

349
00:23:23,569 --> 00:23:25,561
Everything is taken care of.

350
00:23:26,639 --> 00:23:28,733
You don't want me to
drive you to the farm?

351
00:23:28,874 --> 00:23:30,274
I called Mr. Jones.

352
00:23:30,409 --> 00:23:33,777
He says go to his office above the
hardware store, and he'll drive me.

353
00:23:35,147 --> 00:23:37,582
<i>Adios</i> and <i>gracias,
Señor</i> Matlock.

354
00:23:37,716 --> 00:23:40,242
- You are a kind man.
- It was a pleasure, Ramon.

355
00:23:40,386 --> 00:23:41,718
<i>Gracias.</i>

356
00:23:41,854 --> 00:23:45,052
- Good luck to you. Bye.
- Bye-bye.

357
00:23:47,927 --> 00:23:49,361
Oh, thanks.

358
00:23:49,495 --> 00:23:51,088
Did you check my oil?

359
00:23:51,230 --> 00:23:53,290
You didn't ask me to.

360
00:23:54,333 --> 00:23:56,529
No, I guess I didn't.

361
00:24:06,879 --> 00:24:09,041
<i>♪♪</i>

362
00:24:30,536 --> 00:24:33,529
<i>♪♪</i>

363
00:25:31,463 --> 00:25:34,956
Yes?

364
00:25:35,100 --> 00:25:37,660
Uh, Mr. Jones?

365
00:25:37,803 --> 00:25:39,863
- Yes.
- Uh...

366
00:25:40,005 --> 00:25:43,100
My name is Ben Matlock.
I just gave a young man,

367
00:25:43,242 --> 00:25:46,076
Ramon Comaneros,
a ride into town.

368
00:25:46,211 --> 00:25:48,840
Oh, yeah. He told me about you.

369
00:25:48,981 --> 00:25:50,882
- Sit down.
- Oh, thank you. Thank you.

370
00:25:51,016 --> 00:25:52,780
Is he still here?

371
00:25:52,918 --> 00:25:55,114
No. My foreman just
drove him out to the farm.

372
00:25:55,254 --> 00:25:57,223
Oh, dag-gone.

373
00:25:57,356 --> 00:25:59,188
Is something wrong?

374
00:25:59,324 --> 00:26:01,088
Well, he left these pictures

375
00:26:01,226 --> 00:26:03,252
of his family in El
Salvador in my car,

376
00:26:03,395 --> 00:26:04,988
and they mean a lot to him,

377
00:26:05,130 --> 00:26:07,156
and I'm sure he'd want
them back right away.

378
00:26:07,299 --> 00:26:09,928
Well, I'll be driving out
there in about an hour.

379
00:26:10,069 --> 00:26:11,799
I'll see that he gets them.

380
00:26:11,937 --> 00:26:14,099
What kind... What
kind of farm you got?

381
00:26:15,441 --> 00:26:17,603
Well, I got about,

382
00:26:17,743 --> 00:26:20,907
uh, 600 acres in
Timothy right now...

383
00:26:21,046 --> 00:26:24,574
About, uh, 500 in wheat,
uh, 200 in field corn,

384
00:26:24,716 --> 00:26:27,083
and I'm running cattle
on about 70 acres.

385
00:26:27,219 --> 00:26:29,814
Oh, that sounds good. Yeah.

386
00:26:29,955 --> 00:26:33,414
I just retired, and I'm
trying to kind of figure out

387
00:26:33,559 --> 00:26:36,028
what to do with myself
for the next 20 years.

388
00:26:37,763 --> 00:26:39,595
Well, unless you enjoy

389
00:26:39,731 --> 00:26:42,200
being on the brink of
bankruptcy 90 percent of the time,

390
00:26:42,334 --> 00:26:44,633
I'd stay away from farming.

391
00:26:44,770 --> 00:26:46,714
Uh, give me the pictures.
I'll make sure he gets them.

392
00:26:46,738 --> 00:26:49,799
Uh, that's okay. I'll...
I'll take them to him.

393
00:26:49,942 --> 00:26:52,070
I... I got nothing
else to do today.

394
00:26:52,211 --> 00:26:54,271
It's a big farm, Mr. Matlock.

395
00:26:54,413 --> 00:26:58,180
- You'll never be able to find him.
- I'll just keep looking till I do.

396
00:26:58,317 --> 00:27:01,515
I got nothing else
on my calendar.

397
00:27:01,653 --> 00:27:03,383
I'll see you.

398
00:27:03,522 --> 00:27:05,582
- Bye.
- Yeah.

399
00:27:37,589 --> 00:27:40,525
- The rest of you guys come with me.
- You got it.

400
00:27:43,162 --> 00:27:45,927
A terrible thing.
A terrible thing.

401
00:28:13,292 --> 00:28:15,955
What happened?

402
00:28:16,094 --> 00:28:19,690
Jerk said he knew all
about farm equipment.

403
00:28:19,831 --> 00:28:22,995
Expert mechanic, he said he was.

404
00:28:23,135 --> 00:28:26,537
So they put him on a
tractor, and what does he do?

405
00:28:27,739 --> 00:28:29,731
Falls in these disk blades.

406
00:28:29,875 --> 00:28:32,435
Oh, that's horrible.

407
00:28:32,578 --> 00:28:34,046
Oh, they all lie.

408
00:28:34,179 --> 00:28:36,114
They come up here

409
00:28:36,248 --> 00:28:38,843
saying they got all kinds of
experience, looking for work,

410
00:28:38,984 --> 00:28:40,976
and look what happens.

411
00:28:41,119 --> 00:28:44,521
And no more than an
hour after he gets here.

412
00:29:11,750 --> 00:29:13,981
An awful way to die.

413
00:29:15,053 --> 00:29:17,818
Sorry you had to come way out
here just to verify the obvious, Doc,

414
00:29:17,956 --> 00:29:19,618
but rules is rules.

415
00:29:19,758 --> 00:29:21,818
Yeah.

416
00:29:21,960 --> 00:29:23,826
Okay, Jay. He's all yours.

417
00:29:23,962 --> 00:29:25,225
Come on, you crackers.

418
00:29:25,364 --> 00:29:27,230
Let's get back to work.

419
00:29:27,366 --> 00:29:28,766
Come on, boys. Let's go.

420
00:29:28,900 --> 00:29:30,892
So how did it happen, Buck?

421
00:29:31,036 --> 00:29:33,870
The kid told me
he was a mechanic.

422
00:29:34,006 --> 00:29:35,599
So I bring him out here,

423
00:29:35,741 --> 00:29:38,609
tell him to figure out why
this tractor keeps stalling out.

424
00:29:38,744 --> 00:29:42,374
He fires her up, and the next thing I
know, he's caught in the disk blades.

425
00:29:42,514 --> 00:29:45,006
Well, couldn't you help him?

426
00:29:45,150 --> 00:29:47,881
I was climbing
back into my pickup.

427
00:29:48,020 --> 00:29:50,298
By the time I realized what
was happening, it was too late.

428
00:29:50,322 --> 00:29:51,899
- Who the hell are you?
- I'm Ben Matlock.

429
00:29:51,923 --> 00:29:53,949
I just gave that
boy a ride out here.

430
00:29:54,092 --> 00:29:55,958
He seemed so excited
about working here,

431
00:29:56,094 --> 00:29:58,154
and he seemed to know so
much about farm equipment.

432
00:29:58,297 --> 00:30:01,233
- I just can't believe this happened.
- Well, you got no cause blaming me.

433
00:30:01,366 --> 00:30:04,461
- I'm not blaming you. It's just...
- Okay, okay. Calm down, Buck.

434
00:30:04,603 --> 00:30:06,731
We're all a little shook up.

435
00:30:06,872 --> 00:30:08,807
Now, did anybody
else see the accident?

436
00:30:08,940 --> 00:30:10,772
Yeah, one of the
farmhands... Lester.

437
00:30:10,909 --> 00:30:12,537
- He was up in the cab.
- In the cab?

438
00:30:12,678 --> 00:30:14,680
Couldn't he have turned
the damn thing off?

439
00:30:14,704 --> 00:30:15,739
It happened too fast.

440
00:30:15,881 --> 00:30:18,009
Look, mister, this
is a private farm,

441
00:30:18,150 --> 00:30:20,528
and since I'm its foreman and
you ain't got no business being here,

442
00:30:20,552 --> 00:30:22,363
- why don't you just get?
- Buck, take it easy.

443
00:30:22,387 --> 00:30:24,265
You think I like seeing a
man get chewed up like that?

444
00:30:24,289 --> 00:30:25,767
I'm just trying to find
out what happened.

445
00:30:25,791 --> 00:30:28,590
- We all are, Mr...
- Matlock. Ben Matlock.

446
00:30:28,727 --> 00:30:31,094
But you're kind of interfering
with my investigation here,

447
00:30:31,229 --> 00:30:33,562
and I'm afraid I'm going to
have to ask you to move on.

448
00:30:33,699 --> 00:30:37,431
I... I brought these
to give to Ramon.

449
00:30:37,569 --> 00:30:39,504
He left them in my car.

450
00:30:39,638 --> 00:30:41,231
Family pictures.

451
00:30:42,541 --> 00:30:45,101
I'll send them back to El
Salvador with the rest of his effects.

452
00:30:45,243 --> 00:30:47,889
- His address was in his wallet.
- Why don't you keep them with him?

453
00:30:47,913 --> 00:30:49,745
I'm sure he would
have wanted it that way.

454
00:30:51,283 --> 00:30:53,752
Yeah, okay.

455
00:30:53,885 --> 00:30:55,012
Thank you.

456
00:30:55,153 --> 00:30:56,553
E.J.

457
00:30:58,357 --> 00:31:01,953
Excuse me. I couldn't
help but overhear.

458
00:31:02,094 --> 00:31:04,154
Are you Ben Matlock the lawyer?

459
00:31:04,296 --> 00:31:05,889
Retired lawyer.

460
00:31:06,031 --> 00:31:08,296
What an honor. Al Matthews.

461
00:31:08,433 --> 00:31:10,299
So you're... you're
a doctor, huh?

462
00:31:10,435 --> 00:31:12,427
Well, not "a
doctor," the doctor.

463
00:31:12,571 --> 00:31:15,405
If I ever retired, I don't know
what folks around here would do.

464
00:31:15,540 --> 00:31:18,635
I do everything from pulling
teeth to delivering puppies.

465
00:31:18,777 --> 00:31:22,179
But what on earth brings you to Oak
Creek? Not that I'm complaining, mind you.

466
00:31:22,314 --> 00:31:24,840
I'm on my way to
Abbottsville to do some fishing.

467
00:31:24,983 --> 00:31:27,009
Oh, I envy you.

468
00:31:27,152 --> 00:31:29,712
I hear they're biting so well this
year you don't even need bait.

469
00:31:29,755 --> 00:31:32,122
- Yeah?
- Fact is, I know a great motel up there.

470
00:31:32,257 --> 00:31:34,158
The Sundown Lodge.
Owner's a friend of mine.

471
00:31:34,292 --> 00:31:36,337
I'd be glad to give him a call
and tell him you're coming.

472
00:31:36,361 --> 00:31:38,830
Well, thanks. I don't feel like
doing anymore driving today.

473
00:31:38,964 --> 00:31:41,195
Is there a good motel
here in Oak Creek?

474
00:31:41,333 --> 00:31:43,632
Let me put it this way,

475
00:31:43,769 --> 00:31:46,898
there's only one motel in
Oak Creek, and it ain't the Ritz.

476
00:31:47,038 --> 00:31:49,473
You change your
mind, just let me know.

477
00:31:49,608 --> 00:31:51,167
- I'm in the book.
- Thanks.

478
00:32:16,001 --> 00:32:17,902
Mrs. McArdle?

479
00:32:20,138 --> 00:32:22,698
- Mrs. McArdle?
- Oh.

480
00:32:22,841 --> 00:32:24,537
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

481
00:32:24,676 --> 00:32:25,939
Well, you should be.

482
00:32:26,077 --> 00:32:29,707
Someone did that to my cousin Esther
once, and she's been stuttering ever since.

483
00:32:29,848 --> 00:32:32,079
I just wondered if you'd
heard anything from Ben.

484
00:32:32,217 --> 00:32:33,981
Oh, not a peep.

485
00:32:34,119 --> 00:32:36,111
Do you have any
idea where he went?

486
00:32:36,254 --> 00:32:37,745
No, I don't.

487
00:32:37,889 --> 00:32:41,621
But I do know one thing, a
man his age out on his own...

488
00:32:41,760 --> 00:32:43,558
Well, it's just not healthy.

489
00:32:43,695 --> 00:32:46,529
I remember when my
Uncle Clive went out on his...

490
00:32:46,665 --> 00:32:48,429
Did I tell you about
my Uncle Clive?

491
00:32:48,567 --> 00:32:49,967
- Well, I'm not sure.
- Well,

492
00:32:50,101 --> 00:32:53,094
Uncle Clive didn't have it quite
altogether when he was home,

493
00:32:53,238 --> 00:32:55,400
much less when
he was on his own.

494
00:32:55,540 --> 00:32:59,204
Now, one time, he went
to New Orleans by himself,

495
00:32:59,344 --> 00:33:02,746
and he ate one
peck of raw oysters

496
00:33:02,881 --> 00:33:07,285
and two dozen chocolate-covered
donuts in one sitting,

497
00:33:07,419 --> 00:33:11,220
fell in love with a
hoochy-coochy dancer and died.

498
00:33:11,356 --> 00:33:12,356
Oh.

499
00:33:12,424 --> 00:33:14,359
Strain was too much
for him. Don't you see?

500
00:33:14,493 --> 00:33:17,463
- I see that.
- Well, wherever Mr. Matlock is,

501
00:33:17,596 --> 00:33:19,792
I hope there's no
hoochy-coochy dancers.

502
00:33:19,931 --> 00:33:20,931
I hope so, too.

503
00:33:20,999 --> 00:33:23,491
Oh, they can be an awful
strain on a man his age.

504
00:33:23,635 --> 00:33:26,696
Yes. Listen, if he calls, you'll let
me know right away, won't you?

505
00:33:26,838 --> 00:33:29,569
Oh, yes. The poor,
defenseless thing.

506
00:33:29,708 --> 00:33:32,177
Sure hope he doesn't
go like Uncle Clive.

507
00:33:32,310 --> 00:33:34,302
- Yes.
- I lit a candle for him.

508
00:33:34,446 --> 00:33:36,506
Light one for me.

509
00:34:24,129 --> 00:34:25,392
I'd... I'd like a room.

510
00:34:25,530 --> 00:34:27,624
Well, you need
to fill out this form,

511
00:34:27,766 --> 00:34:29,928
and I need to see
a major credit card.

512
00:34:30,068 --> 00:34:31,969
Yes, ma'am.

513
00:34:33,338 --> 00:34:35,000
How long you staying?

514
00:34:35,140 --> 00:34:36,472
- One night.
- Ah.

515
00:34:36,608 --> 00:34:39,578
Uh, two, if there's
a good place to eat.

516
00:34:39,711 --> 00:34:42,545
You'll just be staying the one.

517
00:34:42,681 --> 00:34:45,742
The charge is $40 a night.

518
00:34:45,884 --> 00:34:46,908
Ah.

519
00:34:47,052 --> 00:34:50,216
You won't need more than
one bar of soap, will you?

520
00:34:52,023 --> 00:34:54,652
No, I guess not.

521
00:34:59,230 --> 00:35:01,290
Follow me. It's right over here.

522
00:35:01,433 --> 00:35:04,232
- Do you own this place?
- Me and the bank, yep.

523
00:35:04,369 --> 00:35:07,237
What about your husband?

524
00:35:07,372 --> 00:35:09,102
I noticed you wear a ring.

525
00:35:10,842 --> 00:35:13,175
My husband had a
stroke two years ago.

526
00:35:14,613 --> 00:35:16,775
He's in a home
over in Montgomery.

527
00:35:16,915 --> 00:35:19,384
Oh.

528
00:35:26,591 --> 00:35:28,116
There you go.

529
00:35:28,259 --> 00:35:30,125
Appreciate it if you
took short showers,

530
00:35:30,261 --> 00:35:32,139
and keep the lights off
when you're not using them.

531
00:35:32,163 --> 00:35:35,190
Uh-huh. Where's the bed?

532
00:35:35,333 --> 00:35:37,393
You see that strap?

533
00:35:37,535 --> 00:35:38,901
- Yeah.
- Give her a pull.

534
00:35:39,037 --> 00:35:41,529
If you need anything
else, just dial zero.

535
00:35:41,673 --> 00:35:43,733
I'll pick up eventually.

536
00:36:57,248 --> 00:36:59,774
<i>♪♪</i>

537
00:36:59,918 --> 00:37:01,614
Do you know who that is?

538
00:37:01,753 --> 00:37:03,654
No. Haven't seen him before.

539
00:37:03,788 --> 00:37:05,313
Good evening.

540
00:37:05,457 --> 00:37:07,983
Ben Matlock. I saw you
out at the farm today.

541
00:37:08,126 --> 00:37:10,891
Yeah. I'm out at
the farm every day.

542
00:37:11,029 --> 00:37:13,021
Zacharia Tannenbaum.
Just call me Zack.

543
00:37:13,164 --> 00:37:16,931
Well, Zack, you work out there?

544
00:37:17,068 --> 00:37:19,060
Well, I have been
for the last 10 years,

545
00:37:19,204 --> 00:37:22,902
ever since they foreclosed on
my property outside of Baker...

546
00:37:23,041 --> 00:37:25,840
300 acres of bottom land.

547
00:37:25,977 --> 00:37:28,276
Now it's just one
big subdivision.

548
00:37:28,413 --> 00:37:30,780
Oh. What's good, Zack?

549
00:37:30,915 --> 00:37:33,714
Well, the chicken-fried
steak ain't bad.

550
00:37:33,852 --> 00:37:35,787
And the meatloaf neither,

551
00:37:35,920 --> 00:37:37,855
as long as it's not more
than three days old.

552
00:37:37,989 --> 00:37:40,823
I'll have chicken-fried steak.

553
00:37:40,959 --> 00:37:43,588
That was a terrible thing
that happened out there.

554
00:37:44,596 --> 00:37:47,191
Farming is a
dangerous profession.

555
00:37:47,332 --> 00:37:49,426
Ain't no place for amateurs.

556
00:37:49,567 --> 00:37:51,866
Did you see the accident?

557
00:37:52,003 --> 00:37:53,995
I was putting hay
up in the barn.

558
00:37:54,139 --> 00:37:56,631
First I heard of it
was when Mr. Rayhall

559
00:37:56,775 --> 00:37:58,573
came roaring in to call the Doc.

560
00:37:58,710 --> 00:37:59,973
Mr. Rayhall?

561
00:38:00,111 --> 00:38:02,376
- Foreman. Buck Rayhall.
- Oh.

562
00:38:07,519 --> 00:38:10,648
- Evening, Mr. Matlock.
- Good evening.

563
00:38:10,789 --> 00:38:12,985
- Hello, Zack.
- Jones.

564
00:38:13,124 --> 00:38:15,286
Don't tell me you
decided to spend the night.

565
00:38:15,426 --> 00:38:18,123
Well, like I say, I'm not
exactly on a tight schedule.

566
00:38:18,263 --> 00:38:19,697
How's the meatloaf, Ed?

567
00:38:19,831 --> 00:38:22,801
- Made it yesterday.
- That's what I'll have then.

568
00:38:22,934 --> 00:38:26,598
Well, early to
bed, early to rise.

569
00:38:26,738 --> 00:38:29,884
And that pie ain't too bad either if you
don't mind chewing on the crust a while.

570
00:38:29,908 --> 00:38:30,908
- Ah.
- Take it easy.

571
00:38:31,009 --> 00:38:32,602
You, too.

572
00:38:32,744 --> 00:38:36,078
Doc Matthews tells me you're on your
way to Abbottsville to do some fishing.

573
00:38:36,214 --> 00:38:37,546
Yeah.

574
00:38:37,682 --> 00:38:40,846
Is there going to be a
funeral? You know, for Ramon?

575
00:38:40,985 --> 00:38:45,218
I'm having his body shipped back to
El Salvador so he can be buried there.

576
00:38:45,356 --> 00:38:47,735
- That's very nice of you.
- Well, it's the least I could do.

577
00:38:47,759 --> 00:38:49,887
Such a tragedy.

578
00:38:51,462 --> 00:38:53,294
Farming's a
dangerous profession.

579
00:38:53,431 --> 00:38:55,059
That's just what Zack said.

580
00:38:55,200 --> 00:38:58,796
One chicken-fried
steak and one meatloaf.

581
00:39:18,857 --> 00:39:20,723
Put that thing out.

582
00:39:44,849 --> 00:39:46,511
Something wrong
with your dinner?

583
00:39:46,651 --> 00:39:49,519
Oh, I guess I'm just not
used to that much gravy.

584
00:39:49,654 --> 00:39:52,055
Order something else then,
and tell him to hold the gravy.

585
00:39:52,190 --> 00:39:54,421
No, I'm... That's
okay. I'm full.

586
00:39:54,559 --> 00:39:56,370
You'll hurt the guy's feelings
you don't have a piece of pie.

587
00:39:56,394 --> 00:39:58,886
Oh, I can't. No.

588
00:40:00,098 --> 00:40:02,158
Well, it's a nice time of
the year for a vacation.

589
00:40:02,300 --> 00:40:06,465
I'm retired, Mr. Jones, and
so far it's been no vacation.

590
00:40:06,604 --> 00:40:08,596
Nice to see you.

591
00:40:31,162 --> 00:40:36,362
31, 32, 33.

592
00:40:36,501 --> 00:40:37,501
Hi.

593
00:40:37,568 --> 00:40:39,093
We're closed.

594
00:40:39,237 --> 00:40:42,173
I was wondering if you
towed that truck in yet.

595
00:40:43,608 --> 00:40:46,271
You know, the one that
belonged to Ramon Comaneros.

596
00:40:47,712 --> 00:40:50,978
You know, the boy that was
killed in the accident today.

597
00:40:51,115 --> 00:40:53,584
I thought he come
into town with you.

598
00:40:53,718 --> 00:40:56,847
Well, he hitched a ride with me
because his truck broke down.

599
00:40:56,988 --> 00:40:58,854
I thought maybe
you could, you know,

600
00:40:58,990 --> 00:41:02,791
maybe sell it and send the
money to his family in El Salvador.

601
00:41:02,927 --> 00:41:04,828
I don't know nothing about it.

602
00:41:04,963 --> 00:41:07,398
Hmm. Well,

603
00:41:07,532 --> 00:41:09,443
maybe the other fellow
that works here towed it in.

604
00:41:09,467 --> 00:41:12,096
Ernie don't drive
the tow truck. I do.

605
00:41:12,236 --> 00:41:14,796
He says it's 'cause the air
conditioning don't work no more.

606
00:41:16,207 --> 00:41:17,766
He didn't mention it to you?

607
00:41:17,909 --> 00:41:19,309
Nope.

608
00:41:20,478 --> 00:41:22,447
Maybe he was busy.

609
00:41:22,580 --> 00:41:24,515
Just between you and me, mister,

610
00:41:24,649 --> 00:41:27,175
Ernie don't know the
meaning of the word "busy."

611
00:41:28,252 --> 00:41:29,777
Huh.

612
00:41:29,921 --> 00:41:32,914
Well, thanks anyway.

613
00:41:35,927 --> 00:41:40,058
One, two, three...

614
00:41:49,140 --> 00:41:51,041
Oh, Mr. Matlock,

615
00:41:51,175 --> 00:41:54,407
I'm afraid I misplaced that
registration card you filled out today.

616
00:41:54,545 --> 00:41:57,276
Could I trouble you to come
in now and fill out a new one?

617
00:41:57,415 --> 00:41:58,644
Sure.

618
00:41:58,783 --> 00:42:01,150
The insurance company's
real strict about such things.

619
00:42:17,568 --> 00:42:19,901
No notes? No papers?

620
00:42:20,905 --> 00:42:23,875
Nothing. Just some
fishing gear and clothes,

621
00:42:24,008 --> 00:42:26,603
and me sneaking
around in there like a fool.

622
00:42:26,744 --> 00:42:28,337
I told you.

623
00:42:28,479 --> 00:42:32,177
Ben Matlock's a lawyer, not
some kind of federal agent.

624
00:42:32,316 --> 00:42:35,150
Then why was he asking so many
damn questions out there today?

625
00:42:35,286 --> 00:42:37,646
Well, I'm not crazy about
him being here either,

626
00:42:37,688 --> 00:42:40,886
but I'm inclined to believe it's
just an unfortunate coincidence.

627
00:42:41,025 --> 00:42:43,688
So I suggest we all relax.

628
00:42:43,828 --> 00:42:45,626
Come morning, he'll get up,

629
00:42:45,763 --> 00:42:48,858
eat some of Ed's
Teflon pancakes,

630
00:42:49,000 --> 00:42:52,869
pack his things and be
out of our hair for good.

631
00:43:53,798 --> 00:43:56,529
I'm telling you, somebody
was in my room last night.

632
00:43:56,667 --> 00:43:58,932
Just because you found a
smudge on your undershirt?

633
00:43:59,070 --> 00:44:02,234
This undershirt was
perfectly clean yesterday.

634
00:44:02,373 --> 00:44:05,400
When Mrs. McArdle washes
my undershirts, they're clean.

635
00:44:05,543 --> 00:44:07,637
Who are you accusing? Me?

636
00:44:07,778 --> 00:44:11,476
All I'm saying is there's something about
this town and that boy's death yesterday

637
00:44:11,616 --> 00:44:13,278
that just plain
don't feel right.

638
00:44:13,417 --> 00:44:15,977
And now that somebody's
drug my undershirt into it,

639
00:44:16,120 --> 00:44:18,817
I'm going to find out why.

640
00:44:31,135 --> 00:44:32,295
I went in my room last night,

641
00:44:32,370 --> 00:44:34,805
and I smelled something
funny like cigar smoke.

642
00:44:34,939 --> 00:44:39,843
When I saw that smudge on my undershirt,
I knew somebody had been in my room.

643
00:44:39,977 --> 00:44:41,605
You don't smoke, do you?

644
00:44:41,746 --> 00:44:43,214
No, I don't, Sheriff.

645
00:44:43,347 --> 00:44:44,947
And your hands
ain't particularly dirty.

646
00:44:44,982 --> 00:44:46,416
I didn't say it was her.

647
00:44:46,551 --> 00:44:47,644
It was somebody.

648
00:44:47,785 --> 00:44:50,983
And they didn't take anything?
They just went through your stuff?

649
00:44:51,122 --> 00:44:53,853
Yes, while I was over
at Ed's trying to eat.

650
00:44:58,362 --> 00:45:00,297
Now, Mr. Matlock,
you have to admit

651
00:45:00,431 --> 00:45:02,525
you don't exactly have
an open-and-shut case.

652
00:45:02,667 --> 00:45:04,727
Somebody was in my room.

653
00:45:06,170 --> 00:45:08,730
I can't exactly go arresting
anybody now, can I?

654
00:45:08,873 --> 00:45:10,398
Somebody was in my room.

655
00:45:10,541 --> 00:45:12,203
The Doc tells me
you just retired.

656
00:45:12,343 --> 00:45:14,369
Yeah, but somebody
was in my room.

657
00:45:14,512 --> 00:45:17,539
Why don't you just relax
and enjoy retirement?

658
00:45:17,682 --> 00:45:20,880
That boy that was killed... His
truck broke down on the highway.

659
00:45:21,018 --> 00:45:23,317
That's how come I
gave him a lift into town.

660
00:45:23,454 --> 00:45:26,288
He made arrangements with
that fat fellow at the gas station.

661
00:45:26,424 --> 00:45:27,424
- Ernie?
- Yeah.

662
00:45:27,525 --> 00:45:31,758
He made arrangements with him to
tow his truck into town, and he never did.

663
00:45:31,896 --> 00:45:32,896
Hmm.

664
00:45:32,964 --> 00:45:34,990
Don't you think
that's a little strange?

665
00:45:35,132 --> 00:45:36,725
Ernie probably forgot.

666
00:45:36,867 --> 00:45:38,961
He does that with
irritating frequency.

667
00:45:39,103 --> 00:45:43,234
Mr. Matlock, I was due over
at the Lowell half an hour ago.

668
00:45:43,374 --> 00:45:44,706
Why don't you get
some breakfast?

669
00:45:44,842 --> 00:45:48,040
Maybe things will look different
after some of Ed's gravy and eggs.

670
00:46:45,069 --> 00:46:46,162
Hello?

671
00:46:47,872 --> 00:46:49,431
Anybody here?

672
00:46:55,713 --> 00:46:57,477
<i>♪♪</i>

673
00:46:59,617 --> 00:47:00,778
May I help you?

674
00:47:00,918 --> 00:47:07,119
I was wondering if you could tell
me something about the young man

675
00:47:07,258 --> 00:47:11,195
that was killed in that accident
yesterday, Ramon Comaneros.

676
00:47:11,329 --> 00:47:14,356
I happen to be working
on him even as we speak.

677
00:47:14,498 --> 00:47:15,522
Working on him?

678
00:47:15,666 --> 00:47:17,760
You know, preparing
him for shipment.

679
00:47:17,902 --> 00:47:20,371
Believe me, it's going to
take me all day and then some.

680
00:47:20,504 --> 00:47:22,496
What can I do you for?

681
00:47:22,640 --> 00:47:27,305
Did you notice anything
unusual about the body?

682
00:47:27,445 --> 00:47:29,676
You mean other than the fact

683
00:47:29,814 --> 00:47:32,306
that it was mangled so bad I
had to get an anatomy book

684
00:47:32,450 --> 00:47:35,113
to make sure I've got everything
facing in the right direction?

685
00:47:35,252 --> 00:47:36,982
Okay, a dumb question.

686
00:47:37,121 --> 00:47:41,525
When is he going
back to El Salvador?

687
00:47:42,693 --> 00:47:46,027
Well, I'm sure E.J. has it
written down here somewhere.

688
00:47:51,635 --> 00:47:54,628
I'm sorry, Paulie. Something's
come up. I'll have to call you back.

689
00:47:54,772 --> 00:47:56,400
Matlock's at the funeral parlor.

690
00:47:56,540 --> 00:47:59,510
E.J.'s gone, and that idiot
who works for him is there.

691
00:47:59,643 --> 00:48:01,578
He must might say something.

692
00:48:01,712 --> 00:48:03,681
For God's sakes,
why didn't you call me?

693
00:48:03,814 --> 00:48:05,942
You've been on the phone.

694
00:48:09,086 --> 00:48:13,319
Ramon will be
shipping out on the 24th.

695
00:48:13,457 --> 00:48:14,516
This Thursday.

696
00:48:15,726 --> 00:48:17,251
Excuse me for a sec.

697
00:48:19,797 --> 00:48:21,959
Metcalf Undertaking.

698
00:48:22,099 --> 00:48:23,123
Yeah?

699
00:48:24,535 --> 00:48:25,935
Yeah?

700
00:48:27,838 --> 00:48:29,067
Yeah.

701
00:48:31,809 --> 00:48:35,507
I'm sorry. I forgot. It's against
policy to release any information

702
00:48:35,646 --> 00:48:39,014
concerning one of our customers,
unless you're a blood relative.

703
00:48:40,084 --> 00:48:41,814
That's okay. I understand.

704
00:48:41,952 --> 00:48:43,648
Thanks for your time.

705
00:48:49,860 --> 00:48:51,055
<i>You did what?</i>

706
00:48:51,195 --> 00:48:52,754
I went to the undertaker's,

707
00:48:52,897 --> 00:48:55,560
and I discovered something I
think you ought to know about.

708
00:48:55,699 --> 00:48:59,898
Ramon didn't die till yesterday, but
arrangements to ship his body back...

709
00:49:00,037 --> 00:49:03,565
<i>I'm afraid I just don't have time
for this right now, Mr. Matlock.</i>

710
00:49:03,707 --> 00:49:06,734
But this is important, Sheriff.
I think Ramon was murdered.

711
00:49:06,877 --> 00:49:09,176
<i>I can't get away right now.</i>

712
00:49:09,313 --> 00:49:11,782
<i>If you want to talk, you're
going to have to come out here.</i>

713
00:49:11,916 --> 00:49:13,509
Where are you?

714
00:49:13,651 --> 00:49:18,214
<i>Head down the highway towards
Lowell and turn left on Reservoir Road.</i>

715
00:49:18,355 --> 00:49:21,814
<i>I'm at the Simpson Farm about
five miles down on the right.</i>

716
00:49:21,959 --> 00:49:23,450
Give me 10 minutes.
Much obliged.

717
00:49:23,594 --> 00:49:24,892
You're welcome.

718
00:51:39,229 --> 00:51:41,664
Mr. Matlock, are you all right?

719
00:51:41,799 --> 00:51:43,563
I don't know.

720
00:51:43,701 --> 00:51:47,263
I heard the report on my
radio. What the hell happened?

721
00:51:47,404 --> 00:51:48,929
Somebody tried to kill me.

722
00:51:49,073 --> 00:51:50,073
What?

723
00:51:50,140 --> 00:51:53,338
A pickup truck
ran me off the road.

724
00:51:53,477 --> 00:51:55,036
Deliberately?

725
00:51:55,179 --> 00:51:58,013
Yes, deliberately. Ow!

726
00:51:59,149 --> 00:52:02,483
He kept ramming
me until I lost control.

727
00:52:02,620 --> 00:52:04,020
Take it easy, will you?

728
00:52:05,089 --> 00:52:06,853
What did this pickup look like?

729
00:52:06,991 --> 00:52:08,960
Cream-colored, an older model.

730
00:52:09,093 --> 00:52:10,322
The paint was faded.

731
00:52:10,461 --> 00:52:13,989
It looked like the
hood was all rusted.

732
00:52:14,131 --> 00:52:15,531
I couldn't see
the license plate.

733
00:52:15,666 --> 00:52:17,498
What is that? Battery acid?

734
00:52:17,635 --> 00:52:19,297
Did you get a
look at the driver?

735
00:52:19,436 --> 00:52:22,964
No. But whoever it was
didn't want me to tell you

736
00:52:23,107 --> 00:52:25,076
what I found out
at the undertaker's.

737
00:52:25,209 --> 00:52:27,678
Which was what, exactly?

738
00:52:27,811 --> 00:52:30,906
I found out that arrangements
had already been made

739
00:52:31,048 --> 00:52:36,954
to ship Ramon Comaneros' body to
El Salvador before he was even dead.

740
00:52:37,087 --> 00:52:40,785
A freight order was
placed on the 17th,

741
00:52:40,924 --> 00:52:45,419
three days before the accident.

742
00:52:45,562 --> 00:52:47,861
- You're sure about that?
- I saw the books.

743
00:52:47,998 --> 00:52:51,662
Tell me how this undertaker
knew he had a body to ship

744
00:52:51,802 --> 00:52:54,237
before Ramon was even dead.

745
00:52:54,371 --> 00:52:58,103
That boy's death was
no accident, Sheriff.

746
00:52:59,343 --> 00:53:01,141
He was murdered.

747
00:53:01,278 --> 00:53:02,871
I'll tell you what, Mr. Matlock.

748
00:53:03,013 --> 00:53:04,777
Since I'm the one with
the star on my chest

749
00:53:04,915 --> 00:53:06,993
and you're the one who's
getting patched up like an old tire,

750
00:53:07,017 --> 00:53:08,508
let me handle this, all right?

751
00:53:08,652 --> 00:53:09,652
Ow!

752
00:53:09,720 --> 00:53:11,279
Sorry.

753
00:53:16,393 --> 00:53:18,419
I ain't going to
do no such thing.

754
00:53:18,562 --> 00:53:19,962
Come on, E.J.

755
00:53:20,097 --> 00:53:23,499
You want to look at my books?
You go get yourself a search warrant.

756
00:53:23,634 --> 00:53:26,069
Old Rusty over there let
Mr. Matlock look at your books.

757
00:53:26,203 --> 00:53:29,264
Rusty's brain is so addled from
that rap garbage he listens to all day,

758
00:53:29,406 --> 00:53:31,534
he don't hardly know up
from down, and you know it.

759
00:53:31,675 --> 00:53:33,635
- He's hiding something, Sheriff.
- Mr. Matlock...

760
00:53:33,677 --> 00:53:36,203
I am not, you nosy
old bag of gas.

761
00:53:36,346 --> 00:53:38,372
Better that than
a bald-faced liar.

762
00:53:38,515 --> 00:53:40,143
All right. That's enough.

763
00:53:41,251 --> 00:53:43,277
E.J., if I have to, I
will get a warrant.

764
00:53:43,420 --> 00:53:45,753
So either I see the books
now, or I see them later.

765
00:53:45,889 --> 00:53:47,790
It's as simple as that.

766
00:53:56,500 --> 00:53:57,968
You owe me, Dewey.

767
00:54:03,307 --> 00:54:04,307
There. See?

768
00:54:04,374 --> 00:54:08,175
Ramon Comaneros. Remains
to be shipped the 24th.

769
00:54:08,312 --> 00:54:09,336
Order called in on...

770
00:54:09,480 --> 00:54:10,857
Order called in on the 20th.

771
00:54:10,881 --> 00:54:12,992
I made the call as soon as I
got back from picking him up.

772
00:54:13,016 --> 00:54:15,247
It used to say 17th.
He's changed it.

773
00:54:15,385 --> 00:54:17,115
How could I
change it? It's in ink.

774
00:54:18,989 --> 00:54:21,288
He's changed the
whole page. Look.

775
00:54:21,425 --> 00:54:24,589
See, the shade of ink is
different here from here.

776
00:54:24,728 --> 00:54:26,663
All right, all right.
That's it. Get out.

777
00:54:26,797 --> 00:54:27,890
Take it easy, E.J.

778
00:54:28,031 --> 00:54:30,227
I've been more than
accommodating here, Dewey.

779
00:54:30,367 --> 00:54:33,633
Now get him out of here before I'm
forced to shoot him for trespassing.

780
00:54:33,771 --> 00:54:35,137
Come on, Mr. Matlock.

781
00:54:35,272 --> 00:54:37,617
There's no sense giving E.J. any
more business than he's already got.

782
00:54:37,641 --> 00:54:39,667
Ow! Stop pulling.

783
00:54:50,487 --> 00:54:54,015
He changed the books,
Sheriff. You've got to believe me.

784
00:54:54,158 --> 00:54:55,922
Ramon was murdered.

785
00:54:56,059 --> 00:54:58,858
And chances are he
knows by whom and what for

786
00:54:58,996 --> 00:55:00,624
and probably who
just tried to kill me.

787
00:55:00,764 --> 00:55:03,791
Until we have proof, Mr. Matlock,
we are dead in the water.

788
00:55:03,934 --> 00:55:05,926
I know that, and you know that.

789
00:55:06,069 --> 00:55:08,004
Now, why don't you
go on back to the motel,

790
00:55:08,138 --> 00:55:10,198
take some aspirin
and a nice hot bath,

791
00:55:10,340 --> 00:55:12,485
and I'll see if I can track
down that pickup you described.

792
00:55:12,509 --> 00:55:13,670
Ow!

793
00:55:14,711 --> 00:55:16,441
You can't hit me like that.

794
00:55:16,580 --> 00:55:18,549
You keep hitting me,
and I'll keep hitting you.

795
00:55:31,128 --> 00:55:34,030
What's the matter?
Surprised to see me?

796
00:55:35,732 --> 00:55:37,598
You look terrible.

797
00:55:37,734 --> 00:55:39,293
What happened?

798
00:55:39,436 --> 00:55:42,634
Somebody forced my car
off the road, turned it over.

799
00:55:42,773 --> 00:55:44,901
But you know about
that, don't you?

800
00:55:45,042 --> 00:55:48,740
Oh, you're talking
crazy again, Mr. Matlock.

801
00:55:48,879 --> 00:55:50,313
Now what do you want?

802
00:55:50,447 --> 00:55:52,177
More soap.

803
00:55:52,316 --> 00:55:55,514
I'm going to stay a while
longer. Is that a problem?

804
00:55:56,620 --> 00:55:58,054
Not for me, it isn't.

805
00:55:58,188 --> 00:55:59,952
Meaning what?

806
00:56:00,090 --> 00:56:04,050
Meaning there's some places
some people just plain don't belong,

807
00:56:04,194 --> 00:56:08,131
and if they had any sense,
they'd clear out while they still can.

808
00:56:08,265 --> 00:56:10,598
What's going on
in this town, Alice?

809
00:56:15,105 --> 00:56:16,105
Here.

810
00:56:17,140 --> 00:56:19,006
Because you know what?

811
00:56:19,142 --> 00:56:22,476
Whatever it is, I've got a
feeling your heart's not in it.

812
00:56:24,181 --> 00:56:26,548
That enough soap?

813
00:56:26,683 --> 00:56:27,844
Yeah.

814
00:56:29,620 --> 00:56:30,781
Yeah.

815
00:56:31,822 --> 00:56:33,518
That will be fine.

816
00:56:45,168 --> 00:56:46,582
Why would you go
putting something like that

817
00:56:46,606 --> 00:56:47,914
down in your records
in the first place?

818
00:56:47,938 --> 00:56:50,635
Why shouldn't I? It was a
legitimate business transaction.

819
00:56:50,774 --> 00:56:52,675
Come on, E.J. Use your brain.

820
00:56:52,809 --> 00:56:58,510
Hey, Comaneros was supposed
to arrive on the 17th, remember?

821
00:56:58,649 --> 00:57:03,883
In order to guarantee shipment by
the 26th, I had to put the order in then.

822
00:57:04,021 --> 00:57:06,217
Yeah. It ain't
his fault, Elliot.

823
00:57:06,356 --> 00:57:09,053
It's this bozo's fault for not
taking care of that jackass lawyer

824
00:57:09,192 --> 00:57:10,922
- when he had the chance.
- I couldn't!

825
00:57:11,061 --> 00:57:14,998
A car came along before I could
make sure he was good and dead.

826
00:57:15,132 --> 00:57:16,930
Yeah, well, next
time, I'll handle it.

827
00:57:17,067 --> 00:57:19,468
There won't be a next time.
The state police were there.

828
00:57:19,603 --> 00:57:21,003
They filed a report.

829
00:57:21,138 --> 00:57:22,504
If he has another accident,

830
00:57:22,639 --> 00:57:25,165
a lot of people are going to
start asking a lot of questions.

831
00:57:25,309 --> 00:57:26,971
So what are we going to do?

832
00:57:27,110 --> 00:57:28,976
Nothing.

833
00:57:29,112 --> 00:57:30,808
We keep Mr. Matlock right here

834
00:57:30,948 --> 00:57:34,282
to make sure he stays out of
trouble until the shipment goes out.

835
00:57:34,418 --> 00:57:38,446
After that, it won't matter how
many questions people ask

836
00:57:38,588 --> 00:57:41,581
because all their answers will
be on their way out of the country.

837
00:57:47,230 --> 00:57:49,028
Michelle? Hi.

838
00:57:49,166 --> 00:57:50,166
Hi.

839
00:57:51,268 --> 00:57:53,169
I just got your message.

840
00:57:53,303 --> 00:57:54,464
What's up?

841
00:57:54,604 --> 00:57:57,083
Well, I thought we'd take the
current files back to the office,

842
00:57:57,107 --> 00:57:59,619
and Ben can figure out what he
wants to do with the rest of them.

843
00:57:59,643 --> 00:58:00,872
All right. Good idea.

844
00:58:01,011 --> 00:58:02,479
Want to give me a hand?

845
00:58:02,612 --> 00:58:04,046
Yeah.

846
00:58:04,181 --> 00:58:06,810
So what kind of changes
have you got in the works?

847
00:58:06,950 --> 00:58:08,248
For what?

848
00:58:08,385 --> 00:58:10,081
Business. The office.

849
00:58:10,220 --> 00:58:12,712
We are moving into new
offices now that Ben's retired.

850
00:58:14,057 --> 00:58:15,821
I hadn't really
thought about it.

851
00:58:15,959 --> 00:58:16,959
I have.

852
00:58:17,094 --> 00:58:19,325
Michelle, the office
we've been using isn't you.

853
00:58:19,463 --> 00:58:21,659
It's older,
established, like Ben.

854
00:58:21,798 --> 00:58:25,826
People walk in there, they see
dark, heavy furniture, thick carpet.

855
00:58:25,969 --> 00:58:29,098
They expect to see a gray-haired
fatherly man in a gray suit.

856
00:58:29,239 --> 00:58:30,332
It's not you.

857
00:58:30,474 --> 00:58:32,636
That's true, but...

858
00:58:32,776 --> 00:58:34,836
We need something upscale.

859
00:58:34,978 --> 00:58:36,708
Yeah. Windows.

860
00:58:36,847 --> 00:58:41,308
A reception area with soft leather
couches and a beautiful secretary.

861
00:58:41,451 --> 00:58:43,613
- Conrad.
- Okay, forget the couches.

862
00:58:45,989 --> 00:58:48,151
A new office might
not be a bad idea.

863
00:58:48,291 --> 00:58:50,136
Yeah. And computers...
State-of-the-art computers.

864
00:58:50,160 --> 00:58:51,992
We've got to move
into the 20th century.

865
00:58:52,129 --> 00:58:56,260
You know, Ben's
way wasn't so bad.

866
00:58:56,400 --> 00:59:00,531
Yeah. That was Ben. Ben's
not going to be around anymore.

867
00:59:04,274 --> 00:59:05,606
Yeah.

868
00:59:22,592 --> 00:59:24,117
Good evening, Zack.

869
00:59:26,129 --> 00:59:28,030
Lord have mercy.

870
00:59:28,165 --> 00:59:29,656
What did the other
fellow look like?

871
00:59:29,800 --> 00:59:31,632
I wish I knew.

872
00:59:32,903 --> 00:59:35,065
How's the meatloaf tonight?

873
00:59:35,205 --> 00:59:38,073
I wouldn't know. This
here is a chicken pot pie.

874
00:59:38,208 --> 00:59:40,302
That looks just like meatloaf.

875
00:59:41,378 --> 00:59:42,641
No, it don't.

876
00:59:47,784 --> 00:59:49,343
I'll try the chicken pot pie.

877
00:59:49,486 --> 00:59:51,114
Coming up.

878
00:59:52,656 --> 00:59:54,454
Well, how were things
out at the farm today?

879
00:59:54,591 --> 00:59:56,856
Any more accidents?

880
00:59:56,993 --> 00:59:59,360
None of those, thank God.

881
00:59:59,496 --> 01:00:01,727
About one of them a
year is all I can handle.

882
01:00:01,865 --> 01:00:04,528
Was it a fatal accident
out there last year?

883
01:00:04,668 --> 01:00:05,727
Yeah.

884
01:00:05,869 --> 01:00:07,804
And the year before
that, as I recall.

885
01:00:07,938 --> 01:00:09,634
Same thing happened?

886
01:00:11,775 --> 01:00:15,143
Tractor tipped over
on somebody last year,

887
01:00:15,278 --> 01:00:17,509
and before that, it seems to me

888
01:00:17,647 --> 01:00:19,741
like a fellow got himself
caught in a hay baler.

889
01:00:19,883 --> 01:00:21,818
Boy, that's awful.

890
01:00:21,952 --> 01:00:25,889
Well, it's like I've been telling you,
farming is a dangerous business.

891
01:00:26,022 --> 01:00:28,389
I've been at it
nigh on 62 years,

892
01:00:28,525 --> 01:00:32,758
and I tell you it's remarkable that
I've still got my fingers and toes.

893
01:00:32,896 --> 01:00:34,330
Zack.

894
01:00:34,464 --> 01:00:36,057
Good evening, Mr. Rayhall.

895
01:00:36,199 --> 01:00:37,326
Come on over here, Zack.

896
01:00:37,467 --> 01:00:39,912
I want to talk to you about how
many hands we'll be needing next year.

897
01:00:39,936 --> 01:00:41,837
Bring your plate. This
might take a while.

898
01:00:41,972 --> 01:00:43,838
Sure, sure.

899
01:00:43,974 --> 01:00:45,840
You're gonna have to excuse me.

900
01:00:45,976 --> 01:00:47,467
He's the boss, you know.

901
01:00:47,611 --> 01:00:48,704
Go ahead.

902
01:01:10,267 --> 01:01:13,169
<i>We're sorry. Your call
cannot be completed as dialed.</i>

903
01:01:13,303 --> 01:01:15,465
<i>Please check the
number and try again.</i>

904
01:01:23,847 --> 01:01:25,213
<i>Operator. May I help you?</i>

905
01:01:25,348 --> 01:01:30,116
Yeah. I've been trying to get through
to Atlanta for the past 10 minutes.

906
01:01:30,253 --> 01:01:32,381
<i>All the long distance
lines in the Oak Creek area</i>

907
01:01:32,522 --> 01:01:34,753
<i>are down at the moment, sir.</i>

908
01:01:34,891 --> 01:01:37,053
Well, when will they be back up?

909
01:01:37,194 --> 01:01:39,561
<i>Well, I have no
way of knowing, sir.</i>

910
01:01:40,864 --> 01:01:42,059
Okay, thanks.

911
01:01:42,199 --> 01:01:43,360
<i>Thank you, sir.</i>

912
01:02:01,785 --> 01:02:04,254
I really hate this.

913
01:02:15,332 --> 01:02:17,233
Well, here's your car, Mister.

914
01:02:17,367 --> 01:02:20,929
The transmission is shot,
your universal joint is broken,

915
01:02:21,071 --> 01:02:25,805
the frame is bent, and I can't
even find your water pump.

916
01:02:25,942 --> 01:02:27,934
I don't suppose it runs.

917
01:02:28,078 --> 01:02:30,513
Nope. I don't suppose it does.

918
01:02:31,982 --> 01:02:34,884
Is there anywhere around
here I can rent a car?

919
01:02:36,119 --> 01:02:38,088
The closest rental
place is up in Abbottsville.

920
01:02:38,221 --> 01:02:42,352
But all the long distance
lines are down. I can't call out.

921
01:02:42,492 --> 01:02:43,926
Maybe I can rent your car.

922
01:02:44,060 --> 01:02:45,824
Don't got one.

923
01:02:45,962 --> 01:02:48,158
I could always rent
you my Harley, though.

924
01:03:05,949 --> 01:03:09,852
I guess you won't be going
anywhere anytime soon, will you?

925
01:03:11,621 --> 01:03:13,715
So that's the idea now, is it?

926
01:03:13,857 --> 01:03:16,224
Keep me bottled up here
so I can't go anywhere

927
01:03:16,359 --> 01:03:18,260
and blow the whistle
about what's going on.

928
01:03:18,395 --> 01:03:20,591
I don't know what
you're talking about.

929
01:03:20,730 --> 01:03:22,995
How about renting me your car?

930
01:03:23,133 --> 01:03:26,331
Sorry. I'm going to need it.

931
01:03:26,469 --> 01:03:30,873
Look, Mr. Matlock, you know, we've
got some real good fishing around here.

932
01:03:31,007 --> 01:03:34,136
Maybe you should just
relax and enjoy yourself.

933
01:03:34,277 --> 01:03:36,746
As long as you can't
go nowhere, I mean.

934
01:03:43,320 --> 01:03:45,118
We'll see about that.

935
01:04:33,136 --> 01:04:34,729
Hey, Lucas, how are you doing?

936
01:04:34,871 --> 01:04:36,601
Just fine, Dewey.

937
01:04:36,740 --> 01:04:40,336
Mr. Matlock, we were
wondering what happened to you.

938
01:04:40,477 --> 01:04:41,638
I bet you were.

939
01:04:41,778 --> 01:04:45,112
He seems to think that things
aren't quite right around here.

940
01:04:45,248 --> 01:04:46,307
Come here.

941
01:04:46,449 --> 01:04:48,475
Pardon us for a minute.

942
01:04:48,618 --> 01:04:51,417
Lucas, the guy just retired.

943
01:04:51,554 --> 01:04:53,716
Now, he has nothing
to do but sit around

944
01:04:53,857 --> 01:04:56,850
and try to invent some excitement
where there just ain't any.

945
01:04:56,993 --> 01:05:00,020
Retired or no, he still pulls a
lot of weight in this state, Dewey.

946
01:05:00,163 --> 01:05:01,756
You know why I'm here?

947
01:05:02,899 --> 01:05:06,063
He called the governor. Collect.

948
01:05:06,202 --> 01:05:08,967
But there's not a word of
truth to what he's saying.

949
01:05:09,105 --> 01:05:11,540
I'm sorry. The heat is on.

950
01:05:11,674 --> 01:05:14,041
Now, not only am I going
to investigate this thing,

951
01:05:14,177 --> 01:05:17,739
I'm going to dot every I and
cross every T in the process.

952
01:05:17,881 --> 01:05:19,577
So where do you want to start?

953
01:05:19,716 --> 01:05:22,982
With the accident report on
that boy that died out at the farm.

954
01:05:23,119 --> 01:05:24,849
You've got it.

955
01:05:38,835 --> 01:05:41,361
You're not going
to see for yourself?

956
01:05:41,504 --> 01:05:44,906
The sheriff here tells me there's
nothing to see, Mr. Matlock.

957
01:05:45,041 --> 01:05:46,737
The accident happened
three days ago.

958
01:05:46,876 --> 01:05:49,710
It appears to me that there's
absolutely nothing suspicious about it.

959
01:05:49,846 --> 01:05:51,474
That's because
he wrote the report.

960
01:05:51,614 --> 01:05:53,742
Whatever's going on
around here, he's in on it.

961
01:05:53,883 --> 01:05:55,408
Mr. Matlock.

962
01:05:55,552 --> 01:05:58,215
That undertaker could not
have known to change his books

963
01:05:58,354 --> 01:06:01,654
after that failed attempt on
my life unless you called him.

964
01:06:02,725 --> 01:06:05,194
Well, I've done
everything I can here.

965
01:06:05,328 --> 01:06:08,059
Now, if you need a ride someplace,
I'd be glad to give you one.

966
01:06:08,198 --> 01:06:09,198
No.

967
01:06:09,332 --> 01:06:11,130
Okay.

968
01:06:11,267 --> 01:06:12,496
Well, Dewey...

969
01:06:12,635 --> 01:06:15,104
Next trip, I'll give you a couple
of jars of black-eyed peas.

970
01:06:15,238 --> 01:06:17,503
- I had a bunch this year.
- Sounds good.

971
01:06:32,789 --> 01:06:33,789
You're back.

972
01:06:33,857 --> 01:06:37,817
Yeah. Yeah.

973
01:06:37,961 --> 01:06:42,194
Something just kind
of drew me back.

974
01:06:42,332 --> 01:06:44,892
I made some phone calls
while I was gone, though.

975
01:06:45,034 --> 01:06:49,438
Boy, that's some place you've got your
husband in over there in Montgomery.

976
01:06:49,572 --> 01:06:51,507
I don't know why
you called it a home.

977
01:06:51,641 --> 01:06:55,510
That's one of the finest rehabilitation
centers for stroke victims in the country.

978
01:06:55,645 --> 01:06:56,977
Expensive, though.

979
01:06:57,113 --> 01:07:01,676
It's hard to believe how you pay your
bills on time every month, but you do.

980
01:07:01,818 --> 01:07:06,017
You've got no business
prying into my personal affairs.

981
01:07:06,155 --> 01:07:07,953
Somebody tried
to kill me, Alice.

982
01:07:08,091 --> 01:07:09,184
It wasn't me.

983
01:07:09,325 --> 01:07:11,226
- But you know who it was, don't you?
- No, I...

984
01:07:11,361 --> 01:07:14,820
Just like you know how the undertaker
can afford that big house on the beach

985
01:07:14,964 --> 01:07:19,299
and how Doc Matthews can afford to
keep that big yacht down at Pensacola.

986
01:07:19,435 --> 01:07:21,370
Where did all that
money come from, Alice?

987
01:07:21,504 --> 01:07:22,504
I can't tell you.

988
01:07:22,572 --> 01:07:26,634
You have to, because like
I said, your heart's not in it.

989
01:07:26,776 --> 01:07:30,975
You're not greedy like the others.
Your money goes for a good cause.

990
01:07:31,114 --> 01:07:33,709
So that makes
you an outsider, too.

991
01:07:33,850 --> 01:07:36,820
Sooner or later, the others
are going to realize that,

992
01:07:36,953 --> 01:07:41,414
and they're going to come after
you just like they came after me.

993
01:07:41,558 --> 01:07:44,460
You just leave me alone.

994
01:08:12,722 --> 01:08:14,020
Mr. Rayhall?

995
01:08:15,124 --> 01:08:17,457
This here's Conrad McMasters.

996
01:08:17,594 --> 01:08:18,653
Hi.

997
01:08:18,795 --> 01:08:20,320
He's looking for work.

998
01:08:20,463 --> 01:08:22,864
Since we are a man
short, well, I thought...

999
01:08:22,999 --> 01:08:24,524
- Did you talk to him?
- Yes, sir.

1000
01:08:24,667 --> 01:08:27,187
You're the man who does my
hiring and firing. What do you think?

1001
01:08:27,270 --> 01:08:31,298
Well, if he can do everything he says
he can do, I think we ought to hire him.

1002
01:08:31,441 --> 01:08:32,841
Then I'll give him a shot.

1003
01:08:32,976 --> 01:08:36,344
The starting pay is $6.25 an hour.
Zack will fill you in on all the rest.

1004
01:08:36,479 --> 01:08:37,879
Thanks.

1005
01:08:38,014 --> 01:08:39,573
Welcome aboard.

1006
01:08:48,391 --> 01:08:50,053
Where are you going?

1007
01:08:50,193 --> 01:08:51,354
Fishing.

1008
01:08:51,494 --> 01:08:54,054
I hear it's pretty good
up around the bend here.

1009
01:08:54,197 --> 01:08:56,689
It's a whole lot better
up near Abbottsville.

1010
01:08:56,833 --> 01:08:59,393
Get in. I'll give you a ride back
to the motel so's you can pack,

1011
01:08:59,535 --> 01:09:01,128
and I'll take you on up there.

1012
01:09:01,270 --> 01:09:03,899
First you want me to leave,
then you want me to stay.

1013
01:09:04,040 --> 01:09:05,280
Now you want me to leave again.

1014
01:09:05,341 --> 01:09:07,901
I wish you fellas would
make up your minds.

1015
01:09:08,044 --> 01:09:11,105
Did anybody ever tell you you
were paranoid, Mr. Matlock?

1016
01:09:11,247 --> 01:09:13,648
Sheriff, I've been meaning
to ask you something.

1017
01:09:13,783 --> 01:09:16,753
Those three racehorses
you've got up there in Kentucky...

1018
01:09:16,886 --> 01:09:19,014
Where did you get
that kind of money?

1019
01:09:47,917 --> 01:09:49,385
Hot roast beef...

1020
01:09:50,720 --> 01:09:52,279
hot turkey...

1021
01:09:52,422 --> 01:09:54,357
and one chicken and dumplings.

1022
01:09:55,658 --> 01:09:57,126
What do you mean he's fishing?

1023
01:09:57,260 --> 01:10:00,662
The sheriff saw him in Coral
Creek an hour ago, fishing.

1024
01:10:00,797 --> 01:10:02,425
Why would he go fishing?

1025
01:10:02,565 --> 01:10:05,467
Why not? It's what you said
we should tell him to do, ain't it?

1026
01:10:05,601 --> 01:10:08,594
What did he have to come
around here just now anyway?

1027
01:10:08,738 --> 01:10:10,366
I've got Ernie
keeping an eye on him.

1028
01:10:10,506 --> 01:10:12,407
For the meantime, I've
got a call from Atlanta.

1029
01:10:12,542 --> 01:10:14,582
They want to add to the
shipment going out tomorrow.

1030
01:10:14,711 --> 01:10:16,270
They're making another delivery?

1031
01:10:16,412 --> 01:10:18,574
Tomorrow afternoon.

1032
01:10:18,715 --> 01:10:21,115
With Matlock buzzing around
here like some infernal horsefly?

1033
01:10:21,150 --> 01:10:23,051
Why didn't you just
tell him to forget it?

1034
01:10:23,186 --> 01:10:25,230
Maybe I'll call him back
and let you tell him to forget it.

1035
01:10:25,254 --> 01:10:26,620
Would you like that?

1036
01:10:29,726 --> 01:10:31,388
Well, what are we going to do?

1037
01:10:31,527 --> 01:10:33,086
We're going to
get word to Rayhall,

1038
01:10:33,229 --> 01:10:35,858
and we're going to continue
to keep an eye on Mr. Matlock.

1039
01:10:35,998 --> 01:10:40,197
Yeah? Well, what if he goes
someplace we don't want him to go?

1040
01:10:40,336 --> 01:10:43,306
We'll make sure he doesn't.

1041
01:11:32,655 --> 01:11:35,750
I sure am glad I
brought some bug spray.

1042
01:11:35,892 --> 01:11:37,588
There's a lot of mosquitoes.

1043
01:11:37,727 --> 01:11:39,423
A lot of mosquitoes.

1044
01:11:39,562 --> 01:11:40,655
Yep.

1045
01:11:40,797 --> 01:11:45,735
Boy, I'd sure hate to be somebody
out here today without bug spray.

1046
01:11:45,868 --> 01:11:49,566
Be slapping mosquitoes
and green flies all day.

1047
01:11:49,705 --> 01:11:52,106
Yep. Ah.

1048
01:11:57,046 --> 01:12:01,643
This field here I
seeded two weeks ago.

1049
01:12:01,784 --> 01:12:03,309
Candleberries.

1050
01:12:03,452 --> 01:12:07,184
The field up ahead here, the
one we're going to just now,

1051
01:12:07,323 --> 01:12:09,883
that's also going
to be germinated.

1052
01:12:10,026 --> 01:12:11,995
Which field did that
guy get killed in?

1053
01:12:12,128 --> 01:12:13,460
Right here.

1054
01:12:13,596 --> 01:12:17,829
In fact, that's the disk
that killed him, right there.

1055
01:12:17,967 --> 01:12:22,234
We would move it, but we
can't get the damn tractor started,

1056
01:12:22,371 --> 01:12:26,206
so it just sits there reminding
everybody that comes by

1057
01:12:26,342 --> 01:12:29,972
how fragile and
transitory life is.

1058
01:12:31,914 --> 01:12:33,405
Did you see it happen?

1059
01:12:33,549 --> 01:12:34,881
No, sir.

1060
01:12:35,017 --> 01:12:39,580
But I was there when Lester pulled
that poor fellow out from them blades.

1061
01:12:39,722 --> 01:12:41,247
That was bad enough.

1062
01:12:43,192 --> 01:12:46,356
Ever see anything
like that happen before?

1063
01:12:46,495 --> 01:12:50,262
This is the third fatal
accident we've had up here.

1064
01:12:50,399 --> 01:12:52,425
Three fatal accidents?

1065
01:12:52,568 --> 01:12:53,763
Yes, sir.

1066
01:12:53,903 --> 01:12:55,531
It's a big operation.

1067
01:12:55,671 --> 01:12:59,608
Farming is a dangerous
profession. Just remember that.

1068
01:13:07,450 --> 01:13:10,215
What the hell is he doing here?

1069
01:13:15,992 --> 01:13:17,893
You're trespassing, Goldie.

1070
01:13:18,027 --> 01:13:19,791
You had no right to
get me fired like that.

1071
01:13:19,929 --> 01:13:23,491
I ain't working with a drunk, and I
don't want my boys working with a drunk,

1072
01:13:23,633 --> 01:13:26,330
so just get on out of here
before I have to call the sheriff.

1073
01:13:26,469 --> 01:13:28,349
My drinking ain't the
problem, old man, you are.

1074
01:13:31,040 --> 01:13:32,906
You get back on that contraption

1075
01:13:33,042 --> 01:13:37,605
and get out of here before
I run over the both of you.

1076
01:13:37,747 --> 01:13:38,976
Zack, look out!

1077
01:13:46,923 --> 01:13:49,722
Now, you get out of
here and you stay out,

1078
01:13:49,859 --> 01:13:53,261
unless you want another
whupping and some time in jail.

1079
01:14:05,174 --> 01:14:07,302
Whoa. Are you
all right? Sit down.

1080
01:14:08,344 --> 01:14:13,681
All of a sudden, it just occurred to
me how close I come to getting killed.

1081
01:14:13,816 --> 01:14:16,028
Well, it looked to me like you
were holding your own pretty good.

1082
01:14:16,052 --> 01:14:19,113
He'd have cut me wide open if
you hadn't have jumped in there.

1083
01:14:19,255 --> 01:14:21,588
Thanks. I owe you.

1084
01:14:21,724 --> 01:14:23,693
I owe you big.

1085
01:14:23,826 --> 01:14:25,426
Oh, come on. Let's
go get you patched up.

1086
01:14:35,471 --> 01:14:37,531
Will you be having a cookie?

1087
01:14:37,673 --> 01:14:39,505
Oatmeal and raisin.

1088
01:14:39,642 --> 01:14:41,508
Oh, I'm too upset to eat.

1089
01:14:41,644 --> 01:14:43,579
I hate this. It's just not fair.

1090
01:14:43,713 --> 01:14:46,808
First Ben goes off, then Conrad, and
neither one will tell us where they are.

1091
01:14:46,949 --> 01:14:48,110
You know what I feel like?

1092
01:14:48,250 --> 01:14:50,050
I feel the stupid little
women sitting at home

1093
01:14:50,186 --> 01:14:52,485
while the big strong men
are out hunting bears.

1094
01:14:52,621 --> 01:14:54,283
It's fish, dearie, not bears.

1095
01:14:54,423 --> 01:14:58,087
Now calm yourself.
Have a cookie.

1096
01:14:58,227 --> 01:15:00,696
You're right. If Ben's in
trouble, we should be there.

1097
01:15:00,830 --> 01:15:02,856
Oh, no, no. You don't
want to be taking off

1098
01:15:02,999 --> 01:15:07,869
like those two young women in
that movie, out on the road alone.

1099
01:15:08,004 --> 01:15:10,132
And look at the
trouble they got into.

1100
01:15:10,272 --> 01:15:12,798
We're not Thelma and
Louise, Mrs. McArdle.

1101
01:15:12,942 --> 01:15:15,104
We're just worried
about Ben and Conrad.

1102
01:15:15,244 --> 01:15:19,477
Now, girls, we women have always sat
and waited for the men to come home.

1103
01:15:19,615 --> 01:15:22,449
Haven't you ever seen
the play <i>Riders to the Sea?</i>

1104
01:15:22,585 --> 01:15:27,319
All the men at sea fishing, and
all the women at home waiting,

1105
01:15:27,456 --> 01:15:33,259
and the storm coming up, and the
boats capsizing, and the men all drowning,

1106
01:15:33,396 --> 01:15:37,595
and the women were all wailing
and keening and tearing their hair...

1107
01:15:37,733 --> 01:15:39,497
This wasn't a comedy, was it?

1108
01:15:39,635 --> 01:15:42,503
No, dearie, and neither is life.

1109
01:15:42,638 --> 01:15:44,698
But it's what it's meant to be.

1110
01:15:44,840 --> 01:15:50,143
The men go out to slay the dragons,
and the women wait and pray.

1111
01:15:50,279 --> 01:15:53,772
And there's always the
hoochy-coochy dancers.

1112
01:15:55,051 --> 01:15:57,651
- I have a client in half an hour.
- And I've got to get to court.

1113
01:15:57,686 --> 01:16:00,087
Thank you. You've been a
wonderful help, Mrs. McArdle.

1114
01:16:00,222 --> 01:16:02,020
- Bye, Mrs. McArdle.
- I try. I try.

1115
01:16:03,025 --> 01:16:04,755
Jake, get it saddled up.

1116
01:16:04,894 --> 01:16:07,523
Yes, ma'am. I'll have it
ready to ride in 20 minutes.

1117
01:16:12,902 --> 01:16:16,304
Get that hay in the barn
before the rain comes.

1118
01:16:16,439 --> 01:16:17,702
How's that arm?

1119
01:16:17,840 --> 01:16:21,936
Well, it's awful sore,
but I ain't complaining.

1120
01:16:22,078 --> 01:16:24,877
I'm just glad I've
still got an arm.

1121
01:16:25,948 --> 01:16:28,213
Looks like old Lester's in
thick with the boss, huh?

1122
01:16:28,350 --> 01:16:29,350
Yep.

1123
01:16:29,418 --> 01:16:30,886
Always has been.

1124
01:16:31,020 --> 01:16:33,888
Was he around when those other
men died in the accidents, too?

1125
01:16:34,890 --> 01:16:38,691
Well, yeah, now that you
mention it, seems to me he was.

1126
01:16:38,828 --> 01:16:40,956
You're going to have
to help me with this.

1127
01:16:41,097 --> 01:16:42,531
I'm sorry, Zack.

1128
01:16:54,276 --> 01:16:57,440
If you can't think of something to
do, I can think of something to do.

1129
01:16:59,448 --> 01:17:00,848
Who's that?

1130
01:17:00,983 --> 01:17:04,010
Oh, probably one of
Mr. Rayhall's friends from Atlanta.

1131
01:17:04,153 --> 01:17:06,179
They're always coming and going.

1132
01:17:06,322 --> 01:17:07,915
All right, boys.
We're finished here.

1133
01:17:08,057 --> 01:17:09,855
I'll be in the barn
if you need me.

1134
01:17:11,360 --> 01:17:13,795
You fellows go on
ahead. I'll catch up.

1135
01:17:13,929 --> 01:17:15,659
I'm going to go use the head.

1136
01:17:44,660 --> 01:17:47,596
I wish you'd do something
about all that damn dust out there.

1137
01:17:47,730 --> 01:17:50,359
I'm going to have to go
straight to the carwash after this.

1138
01:17:50,499 --> 01:17:53,401
Give us some more notice next
time, I'll get the road oiled down.

1139
01:17:55,204 --> 01:17:56,797
How much you got?

1140
01:17:56,939 --> 01:17:58,305
Two million.

1141
01:17:58,440 --> 01:18:00,306
Think you can fit it all in?

1142
01:18:00,442 --> 01:18:01,501
No problem.

1143
01:18:01,644 --> 01:18:03,977
Hey, you! What are you doing?

1144
01:18:04,113 --> 01:18:05,547
Mr. Rayhall!

1145
01:18:08,851 --> 01:18:10,410
He was listening at the window.

1146
01:18:14,890 --> 01:18:17,553
Look in the garage. I'll go
down and check the barn.

1147
01:18:26,735 --> 01:18:28,363
I need help.

1148
01:18:37,213 --> 01:18:41,309
Zack, that new guy
Conrad... Did he come in here?

1149
01:18:41,450 --> 01:18:44,284
No. He's supposed to be down
in our disk in the north field.

1150
01:18:44,420 --> 01:18:46,184
- Well, he ain't.
- Wait a minute.

1151
01:18:53,429 --> 01:18:54,590
Come on. Let's go.

1152
01:19:02,738 --> 01:19:04,730
What's the quickest
way out of town?

1153
01:19:04,873 --> 01:19:06,569
My pickup truck, I reckon.

1154
01:19:06,709 --> 01:19:09,269
- What the hell is going on?
- I'll tell you on the way. Come on.

1155
01:20:00,996 --> 01:20:03,329
Caught enough
for you, too, Ernie,

1156
01:20:03,465 --> 01:20:06,458
if you can stop slapping the
mosquitoes long enough to eat.

1157
01:20:20,883 --> 01:20:22,146
About time.

1158
01:20:24,286 --> 01:20:25,618
Get over there.

1159
01:20:26,722 --> 01:20:27,722
What's going on?

1160
01:20:27,823 --> 01:20:29,724
Just turn around.

1161
01:20:29,858 --> 01:20:31,918
What is this all about?

1162
01:20:52,247 --> 01:20:53,613
Conrad!

1163
01:20:56,018 --> 01:20:58,419
Oh, man. Hang on.

1164
01:20:58,554 --> 01:21:00,031
Everything's going to
be all right. Hang on.

1165
01:21:00,055 --> 01:21:02,217
Oh, I don't know
about that, Mr. Matlock.

1166
01:21:03,792 --> 01:21:05,886
It appears to me you
shot him pretty good.

1167
01:21:06,028 --> 01:21:07,496
What the hell are
you talking about?

1168
01:21:07,629 --> 01:21:11,862
The way I'm going to tell
it, you come into your room,

1169
01:21:12,000 --> 01:21:15,129
caught him in here, mistook
him for a burglar, and shot him,

1170
01:21:15,270 --> 01:21:17,762
knowing full well
he wasn't armed.

1171
01:21:17,906 --> 01:21:20,106
You'll never get away with
that. I don't even own a gun.

1172
01:21:23,512 --> 01:21:24,912
You do now.

1173
01:21:25,047 --> 01:21:26,791
The prints on it are
probably a little smudged.

1174
01:21:26,815 --> 01:21:29,046
I'm sure the
ballistics will match.

1175
01:21:30,719 --> 01:21:33,951
I'm afraid you're under arrest
for manslaughter, Mr. Matlock.

1176
01:21:43,599 --> 01:21:45,261
Okay. Get up.

1177
01:21:45,401 --> 01:21:48,269
He's still alive. You've
got to call a doctor.

1178
01:21:48,404 --> 01:21:50,236
Who? Doc Matthews?

1179
01:21:50,372 --> 01:21:53,536
He's busy. At least I'm pretty
sure that's what he would say.

1180
01:21:53,675 --> 01:21:55,109
Then I'll work on him myself.

1181
01:21:55,244 --> 01:21:56,837
No, you don't.
You're going to jail.

1182
01:21:56,979 --> 01:22:00,143
Now stand up before I have
to shoot you for resisting arrest.

1183
01:22:00,282 --> 01:22:01,477
You go to hell.

1184
01:22:01,617 --> 01:22:06,214
Then let me put it this way. Right
now your friend is alive, Mr. Matlock.

1185
01:22:06,355 --> 01:22:08,539
It would be a shame
if in all the excitement

1186
01:22:08,563 --> 01:22:10,520
I accidentally killed
him, wouldn't it?

1187
01:22:13,695 --> 01:22:15,323
Let's go.

1188
01:22:19,501 --> 01:22:20,935
I've got to get my pills.

1189
01:22:22,204 --> 01:22:24,036
What pills?

1190
01:22:24,173 --> 01:22:25,971
For my heart. Heart pills.

1191
01:22:26,108 --> 01:22:27,906
It's beating something fierce.

1192
01:22:28,043 --> 01:22:31,275
If I die, your scheme
goes out the window.

1193
01:22:34,149 --> 01:22:35,173
Where are they? In there?

1194
01:22:35,317 --> 01:22:37,877
In the closet.
Left-hand coat pocket.

1195
01:22:42,658 --> 01:22:44,388
No pills in here.

1196
01:22:44,526 --> 01:22:46,518
Maybe they're in
my right-hand pocket.

1197
01:22:52,734 --> 01:22:54,760
Matlock! Let me out of here!

1198
01:22:54,903 --> 01:22:58,806
Mat... Oh!

1199
01:22:58,874 --> 01:23:00,685
He shot my friend. He's
in there bleeding to death.

1200
01:23:00,709 --> 01:23:03,941
I gave him the key, but I didn't think
he was going to shoot anybody, I swear.

1201
01:23:04,079 --> 01:23:06,014
Then help me. I've got
to get him to a hospital.

1202
01:23:06,148 --> 01:23:07,582
I can't! They'll kill me!

1203
01:23:07,716 --> 01:23:09,207
Whose side are you on, Alice?

1204
01:23:09,351 --> 01:23:11,377
You've got to make up
your mind once and for all.

1205
01:23:11,520 --> 01:23:14,680
Are you going to keep being a part of
this, or are you going to help me stop it?

1206
01:23:15,390 --> 01:23:17,120
- Take my car.
- All right.

1207
01:23:40,983 --> 01:23:42,918
Tell me what's going on.

1208
01:23:43,051 --> 01:23:48,115
A bunch of hoods in Atlanta are
using the town to launder drug money.

1209
01:23:48,257 --> 01:23:51,250
They deliver it here, and then
we smuggle it out of the country...

1210
01:23:51,393 --> 01:23:55,558
Sometimes in shipments of
agricultural goods and sometimes...

1211
01:23:55,697 --> 01:23:56,697
In coffins,

1212
01:23:56,798 --> 01:23:58,767
with bodies of
dead farm workers.

1213
01:24:00,235 --> 01:24:02,466
We get a cut of
everything we handle.

1214
01:24:02,604 --> 01:24:05,564
Sometimes the people who deliver the
money need a place to spend the night.

1215
01:24:05,674 --> 01:24:08,906
I was drowning in medical
bills when this all started up.

1216
01:24:09,044 --> 01:24:11,741
Mr. Jones knew about it,
so the next thing I know,

1217
01:24:11,880 --> 01:24:14,349
he comes to me, and
I'm on their payroll, too.

1218
01:24:14,483 --> 01:24:17,043
Conrad must have found
out about it some way.

1219
01:24:17,185 --> 01:24:20,383
They shot him to get
him and me out of the way.

1220
01:24:20,522 --> 01:24:23,117
Now you hold on,
Conrad, you hear?

1221
01:24:23,258 --> 01:24:24,385
Hold on.

1222
01:24:24,526 --> 01:24:27,018
The money came in
from Atlanta this afternoon.

1223
01:24:27,162 --> 01:24:28,362
Maybe he found out about that.

1224
01:24:28,463 --> 01:24:31,058
This is Thursday.

1225
01:24:31,199 --> 01:24:34,499
Ramon's body gets
shipped out of here today.

1226
01:24:34,636 --> 01:24:36,662
Is Zack Tannenbaum in on this?

1227
01:24:36,805 --> 01:24:38,364
No. Believe or not,

1228
01:24:38,507 --> 01:24:41,909
most of the people in this town
are honest law-abiding citizens.

1229
01:24:42,044 --> 01:24:45,640
It's just the pillars of the
community who are corrupt.

1230
01:24:45,781 --> 01:24:47,716
The bleeding stopped.

1231
01:24:47,849 --> 01:24:49,442
His pulse is better.

1232
01:24:49,585 --> 01:24:51,383
Drop me off at the farm.

1233
01:24:51,520 --> 01:24:53,648
- What?
- Drop me off at the farm,

1234
01:24:53,789 --> 01:24:55,655
then take Conrad
to the hospital.

1235
01:24:55,791 --> 01:24:57,384
Then call the state police.

1236
01:24:57,526 --> 01:25:00,928
Ask for Lieutenant Wells. Tell
him to get down here right away.

1237
01:25:01,063 --> 01:25:03,532
If that body leaves this
town, so does all the proof.

1238
01:25:03,665 --> 01:25:05,827
What are you going to do?

1239
01:25:05,968 --> 01:25:08,597
I'm going to try to round me
up a good old-fashioned posse.

1240
01:25:17,980 --> 01:25:19,812
Okay. Goodbye.

1241
01:25:19,948 --> 01:25:22,213
- When's the truck due?
- Any minute.

1242
01:25:22,351 --> 01:25:23,580
Was that the sheriff?

1243
01:25:23,719 --> 01:25:26,188
He figures Matlock and Alice
are on their way to the hospital.

1244
01:25:26,321 --> 01:25:27,601
He's trying to catch up to them.

1245
01:25:27,723 --> 01:25:30,386
What if he can't? What
if they go to the cops?

1246
01:25:30,525 --> 01:25:33,222
As long as this casket's
gone by the time they get here,

1247
01:25:33,362 --> 01:25:35,024
we've got nothing
to worry about.

1248
01:25:35,163 --> 01:25:37,408
Matlock will just come off sounding
like a babbling lunatic again,

1249
01:25:37,432 --> 01:25:39,298
which means hurry the hell up.

1250
01:25:39,434 --> 01:25:41,198
I don't know if I
can fit it all in here.

1251
01:25:41,336 --> 01:25:42,599
Stick some into the body.

1252
01:25:42,738 --> 01:25:45,640
Nobody's got the stomach to
be poking around those remains.

1253
01:25:45,774 --> 01:25:48,300
Mr. Metcalf, the freight
truck just got here.

1254
01:25:48,443 --> 01:25:49,911
So did we.

1255
01:25:50,045 --> 01:25:52,913
Well, look who's here.

1256
01:25:53,048 --> 01:25:56,610
You always meet at the undertaker's,
or is this a special occasion?

1257
01:25:59,121 --> 01:26:01,113
Get out, all of you.

1258
01:26:02,457 --> 01:26:03,720
You're trespassing.

1259
01:26:03,859 --> 01:26:07,159
I've been telling the folks how you've
been laundering money for the mob.

1260
01:26:07,295 --> 01:26:08,295
What?

1261
01:26:08,363 --> 01:26:10,798
They'd kind of like to
see inside this casket.

1262
01:26:10,932 --> 01:26:13,834
Are you out of your mind?
There's a body in this casket.

1263
01:26:13,969 --> 01:26:15,562
A body and what else?

1264
01:26:15,704 --> 01:26:17,798
Zack, everybody,

1265
01:26:17,939 --> 01:26:20,465
Mr. Matlock's recently retired

1266
01:26:20,609 --> 01:26:23,807
because mentally he's
no longer competent.

1267
01:26:23,945 --> 01:26:28,679
Now, I hate to say it, but he's
senile, and he's making fools of you.

1268
01:26:28,817 --> 01:26:33,517
If he's so senile, why don't you just let
the rest of us take a look in that casket?

1269
01:26:33,655 --> 01:26:35,351
Because you've got no right to.

1270
01:26:35,490 --> 01:26:38,236
Are you going to move out of the
way, or do you want us to move you out?

1271
01:26:38,260 --> 01:26:39,592
- Yeah.
- We will.

1272
01:27:05,487 --> 01:27:08,980
So I'm no longer
mentally competent, huh?

1273
01:27:24,005 --> 01:27:26,804
Our man just pulled Dewey
over out on the highway.

1274
01:27:26,942 --> 01:27:28,604
They're bringing him in.

1275
01:27:28,744 --> 01:27:32,237
I'm sorry, Lieutenant. I know
the sheriff was a friend of yours.

1276
01:27:32,380 --> 01:27:33,609
Not anymore.

1277
01:27:33,749 --> 01:27:35,217
Yeah. Excuse me.

1278
01:27:37,819 --> 01:27:39,879
I talked to the doctors
just before I left.

1279
01:27:40,021 --> 01:27:42,388
Conrad's out of surgery,
and he's going to be all right.

1280
01:27:42,524 --> 01:27:43,719
Oh, thank you, Alice.

1281
01:27:43,859 --> 01:27:48,058
I guess it's time for
me to turn myself in.

1282
01:27:49,331 --> 01:27:51,300
I'll put in a good word for you.

1283
01:27:51,433 --> 01:27:54,460
And thanks again for Conrad.

1284
01:28:08,316 --> 01:28:09,875
You knew, didn't you?

1285
01:28:10,018 --> 01:28:11,281
- No.
- You did, too.

1286
01:28:11,419 --> 01:28:13,320
I didn't know one
thing about this party.

1287
01:28:13,455 --> 01:28:15,117
You weren't suspicious
when Mrs. McArdle

1288
01:28:15,257 --> 01:28:16,901
kicked you out of the
house this afternoon?

1289
01:28:16,925 --> 01:28:18,894
No. She said she was
having the rugs cleaned.

1290
01:28:19,027 --> 01:28:20,638
She kicks me out of
the house all the time.

1291
01:28:20,662 --> 01:28:23,063
- It came as a total surprise?
- Oh, yeah.

1292
01:28:23,198 --> 01:28:24,928
And you're having a good time?

1293
01:28:25,066 --> 01:28:26,329
I'm having a fine time.

1294
01:28:26,468 --> 01:28:28,232
I'm enjoying retirement.

1295
01:28:28,370 --> 01:28:31,033
I had a good time up at
Oak Creek... Now and then.

1296
01:28:31,173 --> 01:28:33,836
Any hoochy-coochy dancers?

1297
01:28:33,975 --> 01:28:35,102
Huh?

1298
01:28:35,243 --> 01:28:36,836
Never mind.

1299
01:28:38,446 --> 01:28:40,005
The easy life's the good life.

1300
01:28:41,283 --> 01:28:42,717
Here comes somebody else.

1301
01:28:42,851 --> 01:28:44,149
Excuse me. Let me go see.

1302
01:28:48,023 --> 01:28:49,389
Come in.

1303
01:28:49,524 --> 01:28:52,790
Oh. Come in, Frank.

1304
01:28:56,264 --> 01:28:57,994
I lost, Ben.

1305
01:28:59,100 --> 01:29:01,331
I know. I heard.

1306
01:29:01,469 --> 01:29:03,529
I shouldn't have fired you, Ben.

1307
01:29:05,173 --> 01:29:07,233
I'd like you to
handle my appeal.

1308
01:29:09,044 --> 01:29:13,106
Frank, I'm retired, you know.

1309
01:29:13,248 --> 01:29:15,308
Come on, Ben.

1310
01:29:15,450 --> 01:29:17,419
Nah.

1311
01:29:17,552 --> 01:29:19,919
I might call you
by the wrong name.

1312
01:29:20,055 --> 01:29:23,116
Men my age get forgetful.

1313
01:29:24,893 --> 01:29:25,952
Name your price.

1314
01:29:31,132 --> 01:29:33,533
Come on in, Frank.
Have some punch.

1315
01:29:33,668 --> 01:29:35,830
The spiked stuff's green.

1316
01:29:38,373 --> 01:29:39,568
Michelle.

1317
01:29:46,948 --> 01:29:49,008
- Guess what.
- What?

1318
01:29:51,353 --> 01:29:53,822
Frank just asked me
to handle his appeal.

1319
01:29:53,955 --> 01:29:55,787
- That's great.
- Are you going to do it?

1320
01:29:55,924 --> 01:29:57,119
You're not retiring?

1321
01:29:57,259 --> 01:29:59,421
No, I'm not retiring.

1322
01:29:59,561 --> 01:30:04,590
I guess I was feeling
sorry for myself.

1323
01:30:04,733 --> 01:30:07,635
I retired and thought
things would be better.

1324
01:30:07,769 --> 01:30:12,207
But I know how to do
one thing... Practice law...

1325
01:30:12,340 --> 01:30:14,900
And it brings me
a great deal of joy.

1326
01:30:15,043 --> 01:30:18,445
And anything that gives
that much happiness, why,

1327
01:30:18,580 --> 01:30:20,947
to give it up, I'd
be a perfect fool.

1328
01:30:21,082 --> 01:30:22,448
You're not retiring.

1329
01:30:22,584 --> 01:30:23,895
- Not retiring.
- You son of a gun.

1330
01:30:23,919 --> 01:30:26,320
- I'm glad.
- Oh, I am, too.

1331
01:30:26,454 --> 01:30:28,923
- Yeah.
- Oh. Ooh.

1332
01:30:29,057 --> 01:30:30,650
Sorry.

1333
01:30:33,895 --> 01:30:35,830
I'll tell you one thing.

1334
01:30:37,198 --> 01:30:38,996
I'm glad to be back.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
